[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
TITLE: Work accumulates on one long-lived branch, restacked into pull requests later
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first restack completes and the branch merges, at which point the next unit
of work chooses its own shape.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
No pull request is opened during development and none is offered. Work
accumulates on one branch until the operator calls a milestone, then it is
restacked into a series of reviewable pull requests.

Two reviews, with different subjects. The stack is reviewed as a whole for
usability, design and flow. The pull requests are reviewed for implementation
choices. Operator acceptance is turned at the second of these, never before.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Commit boundaries are what make the restack legible, which is why worker
sessions commit per unit of work rather than once at the end. And there is no
signature to turn yet: the fingerprints are placeholders until the suspect-link
check exists, so acceptance has nothing to attach to before then.
<<<

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-RESTACK-TO-PRS
TITLE: Restack the long-lived branch into reviewable pull requests
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Cut the accumulated branch into a series of pull requests ordered so each is
independently reviewable, and land them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Slotted as future work so the strategy is tracked rather than remembered. Not
groomed: the cut points depend on what has accumulated by the milestone.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD
TITLE: Reject an AUTHORED_BY flip on a node already merged to main
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A static check over committed trees that reports every node claiming
AUTHORED_BY: human, classified by what a baseline tree said about the same node.

Build a map of UID to AUTHORED_BY from every .sdoc file in a tree -- plans,
docs/spec, and the co-located packages/*/.sdoc alike -- and build it twice, once
at the baseline and once at the candidate. Key by UID across the whole tree,
never per file: grooming moves a node between files with its UID intact, and a
per-file or line-diff view reads that move as a fresh claim. Every node whose
candidate value is human falls into one of two classes. A FLIP is a node the
baseline carried as llm. A NEW-HUMAN is a node the baseline tree does not carry
at all. A flip fails the check; a new-human node is listed and does not.

The baseline is the merge base of the branch and origin/main, not origin/main's
tip. It attributes exactly the changes this branch made and is unmoved by main
advancing underneath a long-lived branch. It moves only when the branch rebases,
and then it moves forward past claims that have already reached main, which is
correct -- those are main's now, and re-reporting them would train the reader to
skip the report. On the branch's first restacked pull request the baseline
carries no .sdoc file at all, so every human node in it reports as new-human;
that is the intended reading of a first appearance, not noise to suppress.

The checkpoint is a CI job checked out with full history. It cannot be a nix
flake check: the flake source tree has no .git, so nothing running under nix
flake check can see a baseline. The same comparison may run in the pre-commit
hook against the previous commit, and there it is a convenience only -- the hook
suite is disabled under CI, and squash merge erases the per-commit record it
would produce.

Permanently out of scope on this side: who made the change. A committed tree
carries no evidence of the actor, so this check reports a claim and never
authorizes one. That is the line with MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD, which sees the
actor and the write it is making and no history. Neither sees both, so an
entitled flip and an unentitled one are indistinguishable to either, and this
milestone therefore ships no override token: anything it could accept would be
forgeable by exactly the party the check guards against. Whether a failing check
blocks a merge is not settled here either -- promoting the job to a required
status check is a ruleset change, which only the operator can make.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
AUTHORED_BY is the operator's key, and the only thing standing against an agent
turning it is a rule in the authoring skill. INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE measured
that class: the sibling rule against editing PARENT_FP failed, and an agent
signed five contracts under the operator's committer identity. Something that
runs is what replaces guidance.

Detection without authorization is worth shipping on its own. The enforcement
actually available today is making every appearance of the operator's key
visible at the place acceptance is turned -- pull-request review, per
DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH -- and a per-pull-request list of nodes claiming that key
is correct without knowing who acted. Withholding the mechanism until it can
also authorize leaves the key unwatched through all of milestones one to four,
and authorization waits on MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS.

The comparison is UID-keyed against a merge base rather than a diff against main
because both cheaper readings are wrong in measured ways: a line diff cannot
distinguish a groom from a birth, and main's tip drifts for reasons that have
nothing to do with the branch.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 on feat/strictdoc-trial at bc646f12, merge base ec980d15.

origin/main carries 0 .sdoc files; the branch carries 9 across three locations.
Every one of the 139 nodes is a first appearance against main today, so the
llm-to-human transition the earlier statement described has no subject yet.
Over the branch's 71 commits: 0 flips and 31 nodes born human, introduced in 13
commits. Creation, not flipping, is what happens.

Why UID-keyed. Commit 4e3f15f4 adds three "+AUTHORED_BY: human" lines: one is a
new node (MECH-PLAN-CHANGE-KINDS) and two are DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE and
DEC-PLAN-SINGLE-OWNER relocating from 02-plan-lifecycle.sdoc into
docs/spec/01-plan-model.sdoc. The UID-keyed comparison reports one. Positive
control: the same line scan reports three on 7f6719db, where all three are
genuine new nodes. Thirteen such relocations occurred across the branch.

Why the actor is invisible here. All 71 commits are authored and committed as
the operator and none is signed (%G? = N for all 71), while 31 of the nodes were
written in agent sessions. The 13 commits that introduced them carry no
Co-Authored-By trailer at all; positive control, the same grep finds
"Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context)" on bc646f12 and 41 such lines
elsewhere on the branch. Commit metadata is not evidence of who acted.

Cost. 0.079 s to compare a 109-node baseline against a 139-node candidate over 9
files (git ls-tree plus one git show per file), 0.063 s against the empty
baseline.

Implementation gotcha. The scan pairs each "UID:" line with the following
"AUTHORED_BY:" line. Both are single-line and field order is enforced, so this
holds under the current grammar and would break silently if the grammar
reordered them. The candidate side can be cross-checked against the JSON export,
which carries AUTHORED_BY for all 139 nodes and agreed exactly with the text
scan (139 nodes, 31 human). The baseline side gets no such cross-check --
exporting a historical tree would require checking it out.

Placement evidence. The flake source store path holds the 9 .sdoc files and no
.git, which is why checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix and
checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix run over ${self} with no history. The precedent
for a history-needing gate in this repo is the gitleaks job at
.github/workflows/ci.yml:718 with fetch-depth: 0. The pre-commit path is gated at
devenv.nix:607 (git-hooks.hooks = lib.optionalAttrs (!isCI)).

Refines SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING, which records this guard as unenforceable until
agents have their own identity. The detection half needs only history and is
enforceable at the CI checkpoint now; only authorization needs the actor.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- Whether a reported flip blocks a merge -- promoting the CI job to a required status check is a ruleset change and the operator's alone.

- What an entitled flip by the operator looks like: whether it is accompanied by anything at all, and what form an override takes once MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS makes one authenticatable.

- Whether AUTHORED_BY may move human back to llm, and whether an agent rewriting a human-authored statement must make it do so.

- Whether new-human nodes accumulate an acceptance record once reviewed, or are simply re-listed against each pull request's own merge base until they land in main.

- Whether the key means 'the operator wrote these words' or 'the operator originated this node' -- the guard's direction is one-sided either way, but the second reading leaves the words under the key free to drift.

AUTHORED_BY STILL READS human AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE IS NOT THE OPERATOR'S
PROSE. It was rewritten wholesale by a model during the 2026-08-27 grooming
pass, which preserved the node's intent and settled its interface but kept
almost none of its wording. The field was left alone because only the operator
turns it. Recorded here because MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD is being built to catch
exactly this, and this pass produced eight instances of it while the question of
what the mark means was still open.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SEMBLE-TEST-RECIPE
TITLE: Make a customized semble testable without a devenv shell
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Expose a documented way to build and exercise a grammar-customized semble.
Today it takes a hand-written impure expression against the flake, and the
invocation must scrub PYTHONPATH and redirect the cache.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured while verifying SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE. An installed semble exports
PYTHONPATH, and that shadows any other build: pointing at a freshly built
binary silently runs the installed one with the installed mappings. The failure
presents as the new grammar not working, which is a false defect report waiting
to happen. It already caused one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CHUNK-LEADING-WHITESPACE
TITLE: Chunks can begin on inter-node whitespace instead of the node header
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on an 8762-byte document: of 17 AST chunks, roughly five begin on a
blank line between nodes rather than on the node header or field start that
follows. Semble's sibling-merging walk treats whitespace tokens as ordinary
children and folds them into the front of the next group.

Cosmetic. The chunk still covers the right content and search results are
correct; the leading blank lines waste a little of the embedding window and
make a raw chunk read slightly worse.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Logged rather than fixed. Any fix lives upstream in semble's chunker or in a
grammar change that stops emitting whitespace as a sibling, and neither is
worth it for the observed cost. A good candidate to hand to an execution engine
later rather than to spend design attention on now.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE
TITLE: Which fields are contract-bearing is configuration, not a constant
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The Nix option surface for custom fields and workflow mechanics exists and the
operator has reviewed the field selection through it.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The set of fields a fingerprint covers is a per-project option, not a hardcoded
list in a script. A prototype may hardcode any reasonable selection to exercise
the logic, but that selection is not settled by having been prototyped.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The selection is the whole mechanism: too broad and every wording change
re-suspects everything until sign-off becomes a rubber stamp, too narrow and a
decision going superseded fails to stale its dependents. That trade is the
operator's to make, and it cannot be reviewed sensibly as an argument in prose
-- it needs the typed option surface the rest of this repository's integrations
are reviewed through.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE
TITLE: Fail safes for fingerprints that reference a state main cannot reconstruct
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A fingerprint that reaches main names its parent's state in the commit that carries it, so main can always reconstruct that state with git show on the commit and the file. This holds by construction, not by archaeology: an entry naming any other state hashes differently from the tree it lands in, and fp-check already reports that as drifted, or as deleted when the parent is gone.

The fail safe is therefore one gate, not a lineage tracker. fp-check runs against the merge result -- the tree that is about to become main, not a diff against the branch's base -- and refuses the merge when it exits non-zero. Nothing is warned and nothing is re-anchored. Re-anchoring is re-signing, re-signing is an acceptance, and acceptance is the operator's act at pull-request review; a gate that silently re-anchors is the rubber stamp the fingerprint exists to prevent.

Squash needs no special handling. It preserves the final tree, so the signature and the state it names land in the same commit whatever the branch did in between. Rebase and conflict-authored content are a different failure -- the parent's content genuinely changed -- and stay with MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS.

Reconstruction, meaning answering what changed for a drift already on main, is a diagnostic and is out of scope for this mechanism. If it is built its shape is fixed: walk git log backwards from HEAD over the file holding the parent, export at each candidate commit, hash the parent, stop at the first match. It must hash from the JSON export rather than from the .sdoc text, or it will not reproduce the recorded value.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The two checks this node used to propose were both git archaeology, and the branch they were written for refutes both. origin/main and the merge base ec980d15 each contain zero .sdoc files while HEAD contains nine, so "every recorded hash corresponds to a parent state reachable in main" and "no recorded hash is ahead of the branch's common base" would each refuse all 31 fingerprint entries on this branch. A fail safe that refuses everything on the only live example is not a fail safe, and neither check can run cheaply enough to gate anything.

The replacement costs nothing to build because it is already built. fp-check compares a recorded hash against the parent's hash in whatever tree it is handed and touches git not at all, which is exactly what makes it evaluable against a merge result. What this mechanism adds is not a check but a place to run one.

Storing the accepted parent state beside its hash was considered and rejected. It would make drift diffs answerable without git, and cost is not the objection -- the twelve parents currently referenced total 7.6 KB of contract payload and all 139 nodes total 120 KB. Redundancy is. A state that passes the gate is already in the merge commit, so the store would duplicate the tree to avoid reading it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 against a clean export of the branch tip.

Today the failure has zero instances: fp-check reports 31 fingerprint entries, 0 signed and current, 31 never signed, 0 suspect. Every recorded value is the placeholder 0000000.

Both controls run on copies of the export, never on the tree. Signing MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS against MECH-FP-CHECK's own current hash 5360cbe gives "1 signed and current, 0 suspect", exit 0. The same edge with deadbee gives "drifted -- recorded deadbee, current 5360cbe", exit 1. So the gate fires on a hash naming a state the tree does not contain, and does not fire on one that names the tree itself. fp-check.py:38 lists drifted, unbacked and deleted as failing and returns 1 for any of them; only drifted and deleted are this invariant's cases, unbacked belongs to MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT.

Why reconstruction is a diagnostic and not a gate: a whole-project JSON export takes 2.06 s wall, and there is no cheaper unit -- strictdoc export against a single .sdoc fails with "error: '@repo'" because the grammar alias resolves only from the root strictdoc_config.py. A sweep of this branch's 61 sdoc-touching commits is therefore about 126 s, and after the squash the intermediate states it would search are gone anyway.

PARENT_FP records 7 hex characters (sdoc_fp.HASH_LEN), 28 bits. If a payload store is ever built despite the rejection above, key it by the full digest and resolve the prefix the way git does; 28 bits reaches a birthday collision near 16k stored states.

MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC MAY be foldable here, and that is a recommendation rather than a retirement -- three other nodes (MECH-SIGNATURE-REVOCATION, MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS twice) still treat it as live, and collection policy is deferred past milestone five by standing ruling, so retiring it is not this node's call. Its premise is that fingerprints referencing intermediate branch states become dangling references the instant the branch squashes; under this gate such fingerprints are drifted at the tip and cannot reach main, so there is nothing left to collect. MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS stays separate: it is about whether a drift report is legitimate, not about whether the state it names survives, and the two overlap only in the sentence "squash is safe", which belongs there.

SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING anchors its CI checkpoint at the branch point and enumerates no merge-result checkpoint. This gate needs one: the tree that matters is the merge result, and a diff against the base cannot see it. Owed to that slice as a sixth checkpoint, not a criticism of the anchor it chose for the checks it does enumerate.

Residual, not handled: a merge that bypasses the gate. Detecting one needs the same fp-check run on main after the fact, which is the same command against a different tree.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- Severity of drifted/deleted at every surface other than the merge gate — command time, pre-commit, pre-push. Only the merge boundary is settled here, and only because the consequence there is permanent and no reader judgement is available. The per-checkpoint severity table is SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING's surface and the operator's call.

- Whether the reconstruction diagnostic is built at all. Its shape is fixed above; whether 2.06 s per candidate commit is worth spending, and whether a drift report citing two hashes is already enough, is a judgement about operator attention.

- Who may re-sign after a legitimate drift. DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY is open and this node deliberately does not narrow it — it only rules out the gate re-anchoring on its own.

- Whether main is swept periodically to catch a merge that bypassed the gate. The mechanism is identical (fp-check on a different tree); whether the bypass is worth guarding is a trust question.

