[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY
TITLE: Who may sign a fingerprint should depend on the grade, not be human-only
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A real drift event produces enough edges to make the current rule painful,
which is the first evidence of where the line actually belongs.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Human-only signing is the current rule and is too blunt. If a couple of nodes
change in a way that touches a hundred edges without meaning anything, and both
ends are llm-authored and llm-graded, an agent should be able to re-sign those
without raising them.

The line is authority, not effort. A contract the operator authored or accepted
escalates to the operator. One that has only ever been llm-graded does not.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure mode of human-only is not that it is unsafe, it is that it floods.
A queue of a hundred no-impact acceptances trains the operator to approve
without reading, which is the same rubber stamp the rule exists to prevent,
arrived at from the other direction.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Unsettled: whether the deciding field is the PARENT's authority, the CHILD's,
or either end being human. The parent owns the contract that moved; the child
owns the assumption that may no longer hold. Both readings are defensible and
the answer probably wants a real drift event to look at.

AUTHORED_BY already carries the distinction, so this likely needs no new field.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD
TITLE: Enforce field ownership at the runtime rather than in a document
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Two fields are the operator's and the tool never writes them: PARENT_FP and AUTHORED_BY. What enforces that is the absence of a code path, not a check placed in front of one. The sdoc CLI derives its field flags from the grammar it loads, minus a named guarded set, so create and set carry no flag for either field; naming one anyway exits non-zero saying who owns it. There is no override flag, because a flag the model can pass is a document rule wearing a different hat.

The two fields need different handling because the grammar treats them differently. AUTHORED_BY is REQUIRED on all five element types, so create cannot omit it and writes llm unconditionally -- the operator's own authorship is recorded by an edit outside the tool, which stays legal for the operator because the harness denial below binds only agent sessions. PARENT_FP is optional on four types and absent from DECISION, so the tool never emits it at all; dev/scripts/fp-accept.py remains its only writer per DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE, and this milestone does not absorb that verb into the CLI.

The guarded set is a list of field titles held by the CLI, not an annotation carried on the grammar. That placement is forced rather than preferred: a .sgra file has no syntax for ownership, and the Nix surface that renders docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra is gated on byte identity and model equality with it, so an extra key there fails a check rather than reaching the tool. The list is validated against the loaded grammar, so a guarded name the grammar no longer declares is an error rather than a silently dead entry.

Three things are explicitly out. The harness denial of direct sdoc and grammar edits is SLICE-SDOC-CLI's own clause and stays there: it is a path-based blanket deny of the edit tool, not a field inspection, and a field-inspecting hook on the same paths would be dead code behind it. Ownership as a modelled, general property over arbitrary fields belongs to the semantic layer along with the rest of the special meaning laid over the grammar (MECH-CONTRACT-FIELDS-EDGE-TO-SEMANTIC-LAYER), which is milestone three; this node is deliberately a fixed two-name list so milestone two does not wait on it. State-dependent ownership is out for the same reason -- an accepted DECISION's frozen STATEMENT, and who moves STATUS from open to accepted, which SPIKE-DECISION-ACCEPTANCE-PATH has not yet run on.

What this does not cover, said here rather than left implied: a model that writes a guarded field with a script of its own. Nothing at write time sees that, and no write-time guard reachable in this milestone can. The complete guarantee is detection afterwards -- fp-check for PARENT_FP, already a flake check, and MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD for the other.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Demonstrated rather than hypothetical. A hard rule in the authoring skill did not stop an agent signing five contracts, and because it committed under the operator's name those signatures were forensically indistinguishable from real ones. INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE records the wider split: every guard that lived in a document failed, every executable one held.

Making the writer incapable of the write is the cheapest executable form and the only one that holds without the harness. Measured: the project settings file that would carry a hook is a devenv shell-entry artifact and is missing from 10 of this clone's 22 worktrees, with no PreToolUse hook at user level behind it. A guard whose guarantee rests on that file is absent about half the time, and absent silently.

An override flag would put the failure back. The incident was a model doing the forbidden thing while holding the document that forbade it; an escape hatch it can reach is the same document with an exit code attached.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The original statement proposed a tool-call hook and claimed it "works whether the model edits directly or through a script it wrote". The second half is false and was measured on 2026-08-27 against this repository's own guard, packages/claude-code/lib/memory-collision-guard.sh. A PreToolUse envelope carries tool_name and tool_input and nothing else: for Write and Edit that is a file_path, for Bash it is a command string. Positive control, same script, same guarded directory, one run apart -- the Write envelope naming the path returns permissionDecision deny with a populated reason; the Bash envelope whose command writes that same path produces empty stdout and exit 0, which is allow. A Bash matcher can only string-match the command, so it catches an invocation by name and not a heredoc. Logged as MECH-PRETOOLUSE-BLIND-TO-BASH.

Delivery measurement behind the RATIONALE's number: 12 of the 22 worktrees under nix-agentic-tools-worktrees have .claude/settings.json, a symlink into /nix/store written on devenv shell entry and gitignored at .gitignore:38; 10 do not. The user-level ~/.claude/settings.json carries PreCompact and UserPromptSubmit hooks only, so nothing backstops a missing project hook. The same gap currently leaves validate-at-stop unarmed in those 10 worktrees, which is a live finding about this repository rather than a hypothetical about this guard. Logged as MECH-PROJECT-HOOKS-ABSENT-IN-WORKTREES.

Test harness precedent: checks/claude-memory-collision-guard.nix, a hermetic runCommandLocal that drives the script over JSON envelopes, gates it through shellcheck -x, and pins the cadence because a deny that repeats is a livelock. A rejection test for this guard should take that shape, with the positive control being the same verb succeeding on an unguarded field -- otherwise a CLI that rejected everything would pass.

fp-accept.py is guarded by nothing today. Any session can run it and one did. The CLI cannot tell operator from agent either: same binary, same uid, same git identity, which is what MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS is about. That is why the discriminator here is the channel rather than the identity -- a harness hook fires only inside an agent session by construction, and the operator running the same command in their own terminal never reaches it.

DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY is open and may later let an agent re-sign contracts that were only ever llm-graded. It does not conflict with this node and is deliberately not a parent: whatever authority rule lands will be implemented by whichever entry point performs the signing, and this node's claim is only that the ordinary authoring verbs are not that entry point. An edge would gate milestone two on an open decision without making the contract any truer.

DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP already schedules an operator review of this milestone's verb list before implementation. An objection to create always writing llm belongs at that review, with this statement in front of it.

