pass, which preserved the node's intent and settled its interface but kept
almost none of its wording. The field was left alone because only the operator
turns it. Recorded here because MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD is being built to catch
exactly this, and this pass produced eight instances of it while the question of
what the mark means was still open.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS
TITLE: Give agents their own signing identity
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
An acting identity is a commit-message trailer written by a hook from the environment. It is not a key, not the git author or committer, and not a field on any node.

The trailer names three things the session already knows. Measured in an agent process on 2026-08-27: AI_AGENT=claude-code_2-1-241_agent gives the harness, its version and the role; CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID gives the run; CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION=1 marks a subagent, which is the kind of actor that signed the five contracts reverted in 9958f190. A commit-msg-stage hook reads them and appends an Acted-By trailer carrying class, harness and run, so the model never writes it and cannot forget it. commit-msg rather than prepare-commit-msg, because the latter's output is still editable before the commit is made. This repository already installs a prek-managed commit-msg hook for convco, so the wiring point exists.

Absence is a third state. A commit with no trailer is unattributed, not the operator's. The operator's own commits are stamped by the same hook from an environment carrying no agent marker, and that inference is only as strong as whatever launches the session. The prek hooks are local-only and bypassable with --no-verify, so the stamp is evidence, not proof.

A verifier reads git, not the graph: blame the PARENT_FP line in porcelain mode to get the commit, then read that commit's trailer. PARENT_FP entries are UID:hash and carry no signer, and putting one there would store a per-event fact in a per-node field.

No new sdoc field, and AUTHORED_BY is unchanged. AUTHORED_BY is the class of a node's statement author; this is the identity of a single act. A rule keyed on grade reads the first, an audit of who turned a key reads the second. This is also what makes MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD enforceable at a checkpoint rather than only inside the acting session.

Scope is the branch: attribution durable across a branch's own history, which is where the review that catches a bad signature happens. Out of scope, deliberately -- cryptographic signing and key issuance; any rule about who may sign what, which is DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY's; and whether attribution must survive the squash to main.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Per-agent SSH keys were the original shape and buy nothing available here. Nothing on this branch is signed -- 71 of 71 commits report %G? = N -- and the operator's git config declares no gpg.format, no user.signingkey and no commit.gpgsign, so this would be commit signing established from zero. It would not be unforgeable at the end of it: the agent process runs as UNIX user caubut, which owns ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 at mode 0600, so a key the agent can use is a key the agent can substitute. Cryptographic separation needs the operator's key held where the agent cannot reach it, and one machine under one user does not offer that.

The threat is confusion, not forgery. The five signatures were written by an agent that misread its authority, not one that lied about who it was. Attribution answers confusion; only a real principal boundary answers forgery, and there is none to be had here.

The trailer is written by a hook because the convention version has already failed at exactly this. The harness asks the model to add Co-Authored-By, nothing in this repository asks for or checks it, and 41 of the branch's 71 commits carry it while 30 do not -- the 30 including 9958f190 and the rest of the fingerprint incident. A carrier the model writes measured 58% on a branch that is almost entirely model-written.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Probably belongs to the beads work rather than here, and is likely to get lost there. Tracked in this plan so it survives, not because this is its home.

Measured 2026-08-27 on feat/strictdoc-trial from its fork point ec980d15, 71 commits.

Author and committer are the operator on 71 of 71, so the committer field carries no information today and is available. It is not durable: 41 of 71 commits already have committer-time != author-time, meaning they were re-created and their committer replaced by whoever rebased. Controlled test in a scratch repo -- a commit with committer "claude-code agent" plus an Acted-By trailer keeps both through a fast-forward rebase; git rebase --root --force-rebase resets the committer to the rebaser and leaves the trailer intact. That is why the trailer is the carrier and the committer is not.

Same test, squashed: both gone, and blame on the agent-written line resolves to the operator's single squash commit. Per-line attribution does not survive a squash by construction, whatever the message says, and this repository squash-merges only. MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS says "Squash is SAFE", which holds for the content hash and is false for attribution.

Positive control for the reader: the same trailer query returned the value on the rebased commit and empty on the squashed one, so an empty result is a real absence rather than a broken query.

Verifier walk-through on the incident line itself -- git blame --porcelain -L85,85 docs/plans/strictdoc-tooling/01-tooling.sdoc gives 9958f190, whose author-mail and committer-mail are both the operator's. The mechanism works and returns the operator for an agent action, which is the defect. git blame prints the author by default; committer appears only under --porcelain, so any check must ask for it.

AI_AGENT is not defined anywhere in this repository -- it comes from the operator's environment. The hook can read it and cannot guarantee it.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- Who may sign what, and whether an acting identity grants or withholds authority. This mechanism makes the actor legible and nothing more; DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY is open and unaffected.

- Whether attribution must survive the squash to main, and what carrier could do it. Measured that neither committer nor trailer survives, so the answer is a statement about what the record is for -- and it meets MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC and MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS.

- The vocabulary of the actor class. The trailer carries whatever the environment names; whether that vocabulary is AUTHORED_BY's llm/human, a finer grading, or something else is a semantics call, not plumbing.

- Whether an unattributed commit should be refused rather than recorded. The plumbing only distinguishes the three states; turning 'unattributed' into a gate is a rule.

- Whether the operator's own commits should be positively stamped at all, given the stamp is inferred from an absent agent marker rather than asserted.

AUTHORED_BY STILL READS human AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE IS NOT THE OPERATOR'S
PROSE. It was rewritten wholesale by a model during the 2026-08-27 grooming
pass, which preserved the node's intent and settled its interface but kept
almost none of its wording. The field was left alone because only the operator
turns it. Recorded here because MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD is being built to catch
exactly this, and this pass produced eight instances of it while the question of
what the mark means was still open.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NO-DELETES-YET
TITLE: Do not delete nodes until the immutability mechanics are settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The retention and immutability question settles: whether a node is deleted with
Git history as the record, or is immutable outside explicit state changes.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Supersede, do not delete. Until the record model is chosen, a removed node is
an unrecoverable choice made by whoever happened to be editing.

Known violation, logged rather than reverted: SLICE-SEMBLE-SDOC was deleted
during deduplication, having been written twice in two places. Restoring it
would recreate the duplicate it was removed to fix, so grooming should rule on
whether to reinstate it as superseded or accept the deletion.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The two candidate models -- delete with Git history as the record, versus
immutable outside state changes -- differ in what a deletion costs. Deleting
under the first is routine; under the second it destroys the record. Choosing
by accident, one edit at a time, is how the answer gets made without anyone
deciding it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GROOM-CONTRADICTION-CHECK
TITLE: Grooming must surface contradictions, not just missing edges
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
When a node is groomed, check whether an edge is missing that should exist, and
whether an existing edge now contradicts what the node says. Surface the
collision rather than silently resolving it.

A live example to test against: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY holds that human-only
signing is too blunt and should key on grade. At least three places currently
assert human-only outright -- the authoring skill's hard rule, the acceptance
language in DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH, and the two-keys split recorded around
DEPTH verified. None of them cite DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY and none is edged to
it, so nothing connects the open question to the assertions it contradicts.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the failure the whole system exists to catch, and it is currently
uncaught in its own graph. The author's stated reason for building this is
returning to a conclusion having forgotten the reasoning; a contradiction with
no edge between its two halves is exactly that, made durable.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT
TITLE: Flows get tests as a contract once they settle
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Once a flow's mechanics are settled it gets tests that stand as its contract, and no regression is acceptable in this area afterwards. Concretely:

What a test is. A scripted run of one flow through the sdoc tool against a throwaway project, asserting what the flow does rather than what any component says it does. One test is an ordered list of steps; a step names the move, its arguments, and exactly one expected outcome -- writes, giving the field values the move must leave on the node, or refused, naming the identifier of the gate that must have refused it. Assertions are on the gate identifier and on the resulting node fields, never on diagnostic wording. A test that pins prose turns a rewording into breakage, which is this node's own rationale arriving one layer later.

Where it is declared. Beside the flow, in the behaviour model of SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL, as a tests list on the flow itself. Not as fixture files on disk: a second source for one expectation is the drift MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE records, and the milestone's premise is that one evaluation emits the model for the tool and the validators both.

Which node type owns it. Neither a new node type nor a new grammar field. A test is data on a flow, so the MECHANISM or SLICE that declares the flow owns it and carries a File relation to the check that runs it.

Where it runs. One generated harness executes every declared flow test and is registered in flake.nix's checks output as strictdoc-flow-contracts, beside strictdoc-fp-check and strictdoc-cycle-check, so nix flake check -- this repository's validation entrypoint -- is what runs it. Each flow gets its own throwaway project and its own --output-dir under the derivation's TMPDIR. No copy in dev/tasks/check.nix: where a working-tree fast path is wanted the task shells out to nix build .#checks.<system>.strictdoc-flow-contracts, which is the one arm of check:sdoc-grammar that already defers instead of restating.

Positive control, per test rather than per suite. A step expecting refused is paired with the same flow run with that gate's precondition removed, which must be accepted. Without the pair, a harness broken in any way at all reports a clean sweep and every refusal in the file means nothing.

The trigger, named in DEPTH. A flow may be built and land at implemented with no contract test. The node that declares it may not move to verified until each of its flows has a test that runs in the check and carries its positive control. That is what "once they settle" means here: implemented is where the mechanics stop moving, verified is the independent pass the ladder already draws, and the requirement needs no new state and no new field.

After a test exists, the flow's declared outcome is the contract. A change to it is read as a contract change, and a test is never relaxed to make a change pass. Whether a flow carrying no test at all fails the evaluation or is only reported in the checkpoint table is SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING's review question, not settled here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Deliberately after settling, not before. Writing tests against mechanics still being played with pins the wrong thing and makes changing it feel like breakage.

The shape follows from what a flow produces. Its observable product is a sequence of writes and refusals, both exactly reproducible from a fixed starting project, so a fixture run is sufficient; a property test would only re-describe the model that generated the flow, and a golden file over rendered output would assert the renderer. Asserting on gate identifiers rather than on diagnostics buys at the wording level the same freedom that waiting for settling buys at the mechanics level.

Declaring the expectation in the model rather than on disk is this repository's own measured lesson. check:sdoc-grammar and checks/strictdoc-grammar-*.nix implement one acceptance list twice, in bash and in Nix, against the same fixtures; they agree today and nothing makes them agree tomorrow (MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE).

The per-test positive control is INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE turned on the tests themselves. Every assertion in a refusal test has the form "this failed", and a broken harness fails everything identically.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Harness precedent: checks/strictdoc-grammar-negative-fixtures.nix. It builds a throwaway project per fixture outside the repository under TMPDIR, gives each export its own --output-dir, reads each fixture's expected layer out of fixtures/README.md rather than restating it, and carries two positive controls because every assertion in it is of the form "this failed". A flow-contract harness is that file with the fixture table replaced by the model's flow tests. The clean output directory is load-bearing rather than tidy: strictdoc export caches under its output dir, and a broken input exits 1 then 0 on re-run against a warm one.

Corpus, measured 2026-08-27 over the JSON export: 139 nodes; 77 at sketch, 16 at implemented, 2 at verified (SLICE-GRAPH-CHECKS, SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE). Three File relations exist in the whole graph and all three name implementation files -- packages/semble/lib/withGrammars.nix twice and packages/semble/patches/extra-grammars.patch -- none a check. So scoping the verified requirement to flows is deliberate: no flow exists yet, so the rule is wired against an empty legacy instead of going red on day one, which is the corpus-first order MECH-RELATION-TARGET-TYPE-LINT records. The same rule applied to every node would invalidate both existing verified nodes on the day it landed.

The File edge from a flow's node to its check is documentation, not enforcement. Measured in MECH-FILE-RELATION-EXISTENCE: a File relation whose VALUE names a path that does not exist exports with exit 0. The harness is the enforcement.

The trigger is phrased against the DEPTH ladder as it stands today. If milestone five moves implemented and verified out of DEPTH -- an open instance decision listed in SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION -- the trigger moves with them, because the condition it names is the independent review pass rather than the field that carries it.

A flow whose gates read git cannot be exercised the way the existing strictdoc checks are. checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix and checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix run over ${self}, a flake source store path with no .git, and neither dev/scripts/fp-check.py nor dev/scripts/cycle-check.py contains the string git or subprocess (0 matches each; the same grep returns 6 on checks/doubled-words.py and finds subprocess at line 116 of checks/strictdoc-grammar-negative-fixtures.nix, so the search works). SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING's pre-commit checkpoint runs against the previous commit, so any flow reaching it needs a synthetic repository built inside the derivation. Logged as MECH-FLOW-TEST-GIT-CHECKPOINTS rather than solved here.

AUTHORED_BY still reads human. The operator's two sentences are preserved -- the first opens the STATEMENT and the RATIONALE's first paragraph is theirs verbatim -- and everything after them was written by a model. Only the operator turns that key (MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD), so it is left alone and flagged here rather than corrected.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- Whether a flow carrying no contract test at all fails the evaluation or is only reported in the checkpoint table. SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING already reserves that same fail-vs-report question for its own review; answering it here would settle a policy in two places.

- Which flows are worth a contract test at all. The mechanism says how a test is declared and where it runs, and deliberately declares no minimum coverage.

- Whether a change to a flow's declared outcome is a signing event. The statement says only that it is read as a contract change; whether it produces a fingerprint, a signature, or a revocation is SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION's, and milestone 5's.

- Whether implemented and verified stay in DEPTH at all. The trigger is written against the ladder as it stands and NOTES records that it follows the review pass rather than the field if milestone 5 moves them.

AUTHORED_BY STILL READS human AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE IS NOT THE OPERATOR'S
PROSE. It was rewritten wholesale by a model during the 2026-08-27 grooming
pass, which preserved the node's intent and settled its interface but kept
almost none of its wording. The field was left alone because only the operator
turns it. Recorded here because MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD is being built to catch
exactly this, and this pass produced eight instances of it while the question of
what the mark means was still open.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-QUERY-GRAPH-REUSE
TITLE: MECH-READY-QUERY's closure walk can reuse cycle-check's graph builder
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
dev/scripts/cycle-check.py builds a UID -> [(target_uid, role)] adjacency map
over every Parent-type relation via sdoc_fp.build_uid_index and a small
RELATIONS walk. MECH-READY-QUERY needs the same shape of graph to walk a
slice's transitive contract-dependency closure and test each node's DEPTH /
STATUS against readiness. Building it should be factored into sdoc_fp.py (or
a shared graph module) once MECH-READY-QUERY is implemented, rather than
reimplemented.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while implementing SLICE-GRAPH-CHECKS: MECH-CYCLE-CHECK and
MECH-READY-QUERY both start from "walk the relation graph from a node," and
cycle-check.py already has a tested version of that walk (verified against
synthetic fixtures, see MECH-CYCLE-CHECK's NOTES). Not acted on now --
MECH-READY-QUERY is still needs-design and this is an implementation detail
for whoever designs it, not a reason to touch it while implementing an
unrelated slice.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-TUI-BOARD-SUBJECT
TITLE: Whether the board renders plans or work
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The TUI's backend is specified against a real data layer rather than fixtures.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The kanban prototype's data model is the earlier overlay's -- TASK, PLAN,
MILESTONE, a work STATUS spanning open through done, and blocking edges. The
grammar in use has none of those: no work-status field anywhere, no milestone,
and node types that describe design rather than work.

A board over the PLAN would reintroduce the work store the plan deliberately
does not keep. A board over the execution engine, fed by the plan at ingest, is
the shape that survives MECH-PLAN-CHANGE-KINDS.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded now because the divergence is invisible while the backend is fixtures
and expensive once it is not. The prototype labels itself honestly -- fixture
backend, no SDoc writes -- so nothing is wrong yet; this is only the fork that
gets chosen when the fixtures are replaced.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Not a criticism of the prototype, which is deliberately unspecced at this
layer. The operator raised the same gap independently.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-CONFIG-HOOK-IMPORTABILITY
TITLE: strictdoc_config.py must put itself on sys.path before defining a hook class
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
On nixpkgs strictdoc 0.27.1, any Format or StrictDocPlugin subclass defined in
strictdoc_config.py crashes every parallelised command -- export with the
default parallelizer, format, manage new, manage auto-uid -- with
ModuleNotFoundError: strictdoc_config in the forkserver workers.
helpers/module.py loads the config by file path without touching sys.path, and
the workers unpickle a ProjectConfig that carries the hook instance. Only
export --no-parallelization survives.

Measured fix: sys.path.insert(0, dirname(abspath(__file__))) as the config's
first statement. All four commands then work, for inline hook classes and for
sibling modules alike. Put the line in the config and in the skill's gotcha
list; drop it once nixpkgs lands >= 0.28.1, whose helpers/module.py does it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured by SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES. Any plugin or Format-seam guard is dead on
arrival without it, and the failure presents as a crash in a worker rather than
as a config error. The upstream fix is the same line moved into the tool, so a
version bump is a convenience here, not a capability.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS
TITLE: Pin formats to SDoc and JSON in strictdoc_config.py
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc_config.py registers exactly two formats, SDocFormat and JSONFormat,
and there is one config, at the repository root. Not a wrapper-only or CI-only
config: `format` and `manage` take no `--formats` flag, so the project config is
the only lever on their cost, and a second config would be two files obliged to
agree about the same thing.

strictdoc.api exports ProjectConfig and no Format class at all, so the config
imports the two from strictdoc.backend.sdoc.sdoc_format and
strictdoc.backend.json.json_format. That couples the config to non-public module
paths on an overlay that tracks latest upstream, and it fails closed: a renamed
module exits 1 naming strictdoc_config.py and the import line, which the two
corpus-export gates already run.

exclude_doc_paths stays as written. Its two globs cost nothing measurable and
re-arm the moment MarkdownFormat is registered again. What changes is the
docstring, which says both globs are required -- a condition that stops holding.

What the repository gives up is exporting html, html2pdf, rst, markdown, excel,
reqif-sdoc, reqifz-sdoc, doxygen and spdx, and ingesting .md, .markdown and
.gra.md as documents or grammars. Nothing asks for any of them: every check,
script, skill recipe and README line passes --formats=json. .sgra grammar
reading is unaffected -- it comes from SDocFormat, which stays.

What it gains, per invocation on this corpus: export 2.02 s to 1.09 s, format
1.50 s to 0.73 s, manage auto-uid 1.70 s to 0.73 s.

The cost is a false green whose shape is narrower than it looks. Asking for a
handle no registered format claims -- html, rst, markdown, excel, or the bare
default, which is html -- exits 0 and writes no non-cache file. It is confined
to OUTPUT. Validation is unaffected: the traceability index is built before any
format is dispatched, so a corpus with a field-order violation exits 1 under
every one of those handles. Both existing gates read their output file
immediately after exporting and die on its absence, so neither can pass on an
empty export.

Making that loud is owed and is not this node's to build. strictdoc's --formats
allowlist is a hardcoded list of built-in handles that a config can only extend,
never narrow, so the guard belongs in the tool SLICE-SDOC-CLI builds -- the same
slice this mechanism blocks. Until then the mitigation is documentary, which per
INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE is not a guard and should be written as one that does
not yet execute.

Explicitly out of scope: making HTML render. This node does not repair it, gate
it, or decide whether it should exist -- MECH-DEFAULT-EXPORT-IS-RED-ON-RST is
the instance, MECH-HTML-RENDER-MUST-PASS the requirement. Re-registering
HTMLFormat is that node's change and a one-line revert here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The traceability index build is 3 ms; the cost of every strictdoc command is
startup, config construction and the format stack default_formats() imports.
This is the single largest lever on the skill loop and on every validation tier,
and it needs no package change.

The fork this node ended on -- pin the formats, or keep HTMLFormat and pay for
it -- collapses on measurement, in both directions.

Keeping HTMLFormat preserves no working HTML export. With HTMLFormat registered,
`strictdoc export . --formats=html` exits 1 on this corpus, aborting partway with
113 files written, because a bare glob in one node's prose is an unterminated RST
emphasis marker. That arm buys a red render for 0.48 s of every command, and the
operator has already ruled the breakage low-priority tech debt that must not
block unrelated work.

Dropping it opens no validation hole. The dangerous reading of the false green --
that the loop the skill documents as "export == validate" would report green on a
broken graph -- is false. Validation happens in the index build, upstream of
format dispatch.

What is left is a real but bounded output trap, in a repository where nothing
invokes an unregistered handle and both gates would fail if one did. Against
roughly halving every strictdoc invocation, on the commands every authoring loop
and both CI checks run, that trades well.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Re-measured 2026-08-27 against strictdoc 0.28.3, cold cache (clean --output-dir
per run, since the cache lives at <output-dir>/_cache/<version>), on a copy of
the worktree. Interleaved A/B/C rounds, four of them:

  export --formats=json   stock 1.98-2.12 s  lean 1.08-1.13 s  lean+HTML 1.55-1.58 s
  export --no-parallelization --formats=json  stock 2.14-2.28 s  lean 1.31 s (x3)
  format .                stock 1.48-1.97 s  lean 0.70-1.18 s
  manage auto-uid .       stock 1.63-1.78 s  lean 0.71-0.74 s

JSON output is byte-identical across stock, lean and lean+HTML (diff -r over the
json/ tree), and identical again with exclude_doc_paths removed under lean.

FOUR CORRECTIONS to the numbers this node previously carried, all from the
re-measurement above.

The absolute timings do not reproduce. This node said export 3.2 s to 1.24 s;
measured 2.02 s to 1.09 s. The RATIO holds -- pinning roughly halves every
command -- the absolutes do not, so quote the ratio.

The claim that "the forkserver parallelizer adds about 1.4 s" is REFUTED.
--no-parallelization is SLOWER than parallel in both configs, by about 0.15 s
stock and 0.22 s lean. There is no 1.4 s parallelizer tax to recover, and
--no-parallelization is not a lever.

"65 nodes" is stale: the corpus is 139 UIDs across 9 documents.

The fork's second arm was priced at 0.6 s for HTMLFormat; measured 0.48 s
(2.02 vs 1.55). More to the point, what it buys exits 1.

MEASURED, on the false green. Under lean, --formats=html, rst, markdown, excel
and the bare default all exit 0 with zero non-cache files written; the 367 files
in the output dir are all _cache. --formats=json,html writes json/ and exits 0.
Positive control, same binary, same command, HTMLFormat registered: 26 html
files then exit 1 on this corpus, and on a clean two-node mini corpus 5 html
files and exit 0. So "writes nothing" is a property of the format registry, not
of the broken prose.

Second positive control, on the validation half: a field-order violation
injected into 99-backlog.sdoc exits 1 under lean for the bare default, for
--formats=json and for --formats=html alike, with "Semantic error: Wrong field
order". Third: fp-check.py and cycle-check.py both traceback and exit 1 on a
missing index.json, so an empty export cannot pass either gate.

MEASURED, on the markdown half. With MarkdownFormat unregistered, deleting
exclude_doc_paths changes nothing: exit 0, JSON byte-identical, no measurable
time difference (1.06-1.19 s against 1.04-1.20 s). Positive control with the
full format stack and the globs deleted: exit 1, "Reading SDOC: 281 tasks", then
"A process in the process pool was terminated abruptly". That crash is the
reason the globs stay -- they are free, and they are the difference between a
clean export and an unreadable pool failure if MarkdownFormat ever returns.

Upstream mechanics worth not re-deriving. default_formats() (project_config.py
:572) returns 13 Format instances and is used whenever the config passes no
formats= (:411). export_formats defaults to ["html"] (:723), which is why bare
`export` is an html request. ExportAction iterates the REGISTERED formats and
matches them against the requested handles (export_action.py:91), so an
unclaimed handle is a no-op loop -- while needs_html_templates is gated on the
REQUESTED handle (:53), so --formats=html still compiles Jinja templates for
0.53 s before writing nothing. build_index() runs in ExportAction.__init__
(:27), which is why validation is upstream of all of this. EXPORT_FORMATS
(commands/export.py:17) is the hardcoded argparse allowlist and config formats
only append to it (:88), so no config can make --formats=html an argparse error.
DocumentFinder builds its extension table from the registered formats
(document_finder.py:41), which is the whole markdown-ingestion effect.

PROPAGATION, in the same commit. Landing this makes the first sentence of
MECH-DEFAULT-EXPORT-IS-RED-ON-RST false -- bare `strictdoc export .` stops
exiting 1 and starts exiting 0 having written nothing -- so that node's
STATEMENT changes with this one. The config docstring's "BOTH globs are
required" changes too.

A minor lever, measured and not required: importing ProjectConfig from
strictdoc.core.project_config rather than strictdoc.api saves about 0.06 s
(1.06 s against 1.12 s), because strictdoc.api's __init__ imports ExcelGenerator
and so drags openpyxl in even when no Excel format is registered. Under the
stock format list it saves nothing, default_formats() importing the same modules
a moment later.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE
TITLE: One readiness predicate, consumed by every script that asks
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Readiness is defined in four places, read at two scopes, and the four disagree. dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py:100 is_ready is the node-local rule fp-accept consults before signing a fingerprint. docs/sdoc/status.py:58 closure_verdict re-implements the same rule inline while walking a slice's closure. dev/skills/sdoc/SKILL.md:77 and MECH-READY-QUERY state it in prose, identically to each other and differently from both executables. DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY is the contract all four claim to implement and enumerates no state at all.

Measured disagreement, four cells. A rejected decision: both executables pass it, both prose readings block it. A superseded decision: fp-accept signs against it, status.py blocks. A SPIKE whose STATUS is unrun or blocked: every reading treats it as settled on DEPTH alone, because no gate consults a spike's STATUS. A suspect edge: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY makes it part of the predicate, and status.py never computes one because it never calls fp-check.

The dependency edge set is defined twice with complementary filters -- status.py:47 takes every relation whose TYPE is not File, sdoc_fp.py:97 takes every relation whose TYPE is Parent -- and the DEPTH ladder is written three times in two languages: packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix:35, status.py:33, sdoc_fp.py:38.

The deliverable is one read point, not a ruling. The predicate and the edge-set live in dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py, which cycle-check.py already imports by the sys.path.insert idiom the dev/scripts carry; status.py and render.py gain that import rather than moving, because checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix and checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix name dev/scripts/ paths while the skill names docs/sdoc/ paths. It exports four things, and nothing outside it decides readiness:

STATE_VERDICTS -- a table from one state-field value to settled, blocks or freedom. Seven rows: one per DECISION STATUS, one per SPIKE STATUS, plus the cut point on the DEPTH ladder. Its contents are milestone 5's, listed below.

settled(node) -> (bool, reason) -- the node-local question fp-accept asks of a single parent, answered from the table.

dependencies(node) -> set of UID -- the one edge-set: every relation whose TYPE is Parent, plus every PARENT_FP parent. TYPE is Parent, not "is not File": a Child relation points downstream and is not a dependency, and status.py's complement filter would walk one into the closure the day the grammar declares one.

suspect_edges(index) -> set of (node, parent) -- fp-check.py:42's drifted, unbacked and deleted findings lifted out so a closure walk can apply the no-suspect-edge condition status.py omits. never-signed is not suspect.

After the change fp-accept calls settled, fp-check calls suspect_edges, status.py's closure_verdict calls all four and holds no rule of its own, and MECH-READY-QUERY calls the same four when it lands. cycle-check.py's build_graph moves here too. It filters TYPE == Parent, which is the SAME edge set and the same direction as sdoc_fp.relation_parent_uids and differs only by carrying the ROLE alongside the target, so it takes a flag for whether the role travels and another for whether PARENT_FP parents count as edges. render.py computes no readiness and only imports the fingerprint placeholder.

Behaviour is held constant except where a gate tightens. The shipped table transcribes status.py's current reading, the stricter of the two executables on the one cell where they differ, so fp-accept stops signing against a superseded parent and no other output moves. Transcribing rather than ruling is the point: which states are settled is not this mechanism's to decide.

Left blank for milestone 5, one cell each: whether rejected blocks; whether superseded blocks; whether a decision that is neither open nor accepted contributes a degree of freedom; whether a SPIKE's STATUS gates at all and which of unrun and blocked blocks; and whether no-suspect-edge belongs in the predicate or in a separate report. When SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL's evaluation emits its machine-readable model, the table becomes a projection of it and stops being a literal.

Done when all five scripts produce byte-identical output on the current corpus, except fp-accept's superseded refusal, and the injected-state control below is re-run against the module.
<<<
RATIONALE: Two executables carrying two readings of one rule is the document-borne drift INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE names, and the rule is the one part of this that should not be code: it is lifecycle policy, which DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP puts in milestone 5. Separating the read point from the rule now means milestone 5 fills a seven-cell table instead of editing four scripts in two directories, and means the disagreement is inspectable as data rather than distributed across two hand-copied constants and two inline conditionals. Deferring the extraction until milestone 5 inverts that: the migration would arrive to find the thing it migrates written four times.
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 against a clean export at bc646f12, BEFORE that day's grooming moved twenty nodes (rm -rf of the output dir first; incremental export produces false greens). The counts below are that snapshot and are not current; the shape of the finding is what carries, not the arithmetic. 139 nodes then. Relation TYPE census: Parent 92, File 3, Child 0. DECISION STATUS: 13 accepted, 7 open, 1 superseded, 0 rejected. DEPTH: 77 sketch, 16 implemented, 14 interface-settled, 9 needs-design, 2 verified, 21 decisions carrying none. Fingerprints: 31 entries, 31 never-signed, 0 suspect -- so no-suspect-edge is vacuously true today and starts to matter at the first signed fingerprint.

How much of the divergence is latent, and how much is not. Latent: zero rejected decisions exist, and the one superseded decision, DEC-VIEWER-BEFORE-BULK, has zero inbound relations and is named by zero PARENT_FP entries. Not latent: SPIKE-CONVERSION-SIGNAL is STATUS unrun at DEPTH interface-settled, so both predicates already count an unrun spike as settled wherever it appears in a closure. Also not yet live but one grammar edit away: packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/dsl.nix:123 already offers rel.child, and values.nix:8 records the child relation as surface this repository does not exercise.

Positive control, run in memory over a deep copy of the export with no file written. Setting DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION's STATUS to each of the four values in turn: open -> fp-accept refuses and status.py blocks; accepted -> fp-accept signs and status.py passes; rejected -> fp-accept signs and status.py passes; superseded -> fp-accept signs and status.py blocks. The control detects agreement as well as disagreement, so the one divergent row is not an artefact of the harness. Setting SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES's STATUS to unrun, run and blocked in turn changes neither answer. Appending one TYPE Child relation to MECH-CYCLE-CHECK makes status.py's dependencies return two UIDs where sdoc_fp's relation_parent_uids returns one.

Three implementations of node discovery also exist -- the recursive UID-prefix walk in status.py:20, the same walk in render.py:27, and the DOCUMENTS/NODES walk keyed on _NODE_TYPE in sdoc_fp.py:45 -- and all three returned the same 139 nodes at that snapshot. They are out of scope here and logged separately; the count is recorded so a later divergence is legible as a change rather than a discovery.

Sequencing. MECH-READY-QUERY and this node are not the same work and do not merge: this one produces the predicate and the edge-set, that one the closure query and the bounded implementation prompt built from the same traversal. MECH-READINESS-REPORT-FRONTIER is a different case -- both of its complaints, a root reporting itself as its own blocker and an undifferentiated blocker list, are answered by MECH-READY-QUERY's record, so it closes as subsumed when that lands. This one lands first and changes no output; the other two consume it. MECH-READY-QUERY carries only a Governed_By edge to DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY and has no edge recording that dependency.

Correction to this node's earlier statement, kept so the change is legible. It said five definitions exist and no two agree. There are four readings, not five -- DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY is the contract the other four implement, not a fifth reading of it -- and two pairs do agree: the skill and MECH-READY-QUERY word it identically, and on rejected the two executables agree by both passing it. The executables differ in exactly one cell, superseded. It also cited SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE being verified while status.py reports it blocked on DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION; that reproduces exactly, but both predicates answer the same there, so it is DEPTH and closure-readiness being independent axes rather than two predicates disagreeing, and it is already recorded verbatim in MECH-READY-QUERY's own notes.

LEFT TO THE OPERATOR, deliberately. This milestone settles the plumbing;
each of these is a rule about how this instance works and milestone five is
where they get written.

- Whether a `rejected` decision in a closure blocks. Both executables currently pass it; both prose readings block it. Zero instances exist today.

- Whether a `superseded` decision blocks. This is the single cell where the two executables actually disagree today. The one superseded decision has no dependents.

- Whether a decision that is neither open nor accepted contributes to the degrees-of-freedom count, or only to the blocker list.

- Whether a SPIKE's STATUS gates at all, and if so which of `unrun` and `blocked` blocks. DEPTH describes a spike's design maturity and STATUS its execution; nothing today says whether an unrun spike may sit in a settled closure.

- Whether no-suspect-edge is part of the readiness predicate, as DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY words it, or a separate report status.py prints alongside its verdict.

- Whether the DEPTH cut point stays at interface-settled for every node type, or differs per type.

- The shipped table transcribes status.py's reading as a placeholder so no gate loosens. That transcription is not a ruling on any cell above and is expected to be replaced wholesale at milestone 5.
<<<
