[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-EDIT-VIA-MODEL
TITLE: Replace sdoc_edit.py's hand-copied field order with strictdoc's node model
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Replace dev/scripts/sdoc_edit.py with dev/scripts/sdoc_model.py, a module over
strictdoc's node model that owns a loaded graph. Three operations:
open_graph(root: Path, *, output_dir: Path | None = None) -> Graph,
Graph.set_field(uid: str, field: str, value: str | None) -> None, and
Graph.save() -> list[Path] returning the files rewritten. set_field validates
before it returns and raises rather than leaving a bad node in the graph; save
writes only the documents whose nodes were touched. Batching is the point: the
graph load is the expensive step and fp-accept signs several UIDs per run.
FIELD_ORDER, find_node_block, find_field_span and render_field all go, and
nothing in the replacement knows the grammar's field order or its block syntax.

The call sequence is fixed by what strictdoc requires. Load with
ProjectConfigLoader.load(input_path=root, output_dir=...) then
TraceabilityIndexBuilder.create(project_config=..., parallelizer=
NullParallelizer(), skip_source_files=True). Reach a node with
index.get_node_by_uid(uid) and its file with
node.get_document().meta.input_doc_full_path. Write a field with
SDocNode.set_field_value(field_name=..., form_field_index=0, value=...), where
value=None deletes the field. Check the node with SDocValidator.validate_node(
node, document_grammar=doc.grammar, path_to_sdoc_file=...). Serialise with
SDWriter(project_config).write(document) and replace the file with that string.

Three obligations sit on the module because strictdoc does not carry them.
Value validation: set_field_value accepts anything, so an invalid SingleChoice
and the deletion of a required field both reach disk and are caught only by a
later parse -- validate_node is the catchable gate and costs about 20 us.
Delimiter rejection: a value containing a line that begins at column zero with
"<<<" serialises to a file that cannot be re-parsed, and validate_node does not
see it, so set_field rejects such values itself. Path discovery: the model
supplies each node's source file directly, replacing fp-accept's glob-and-grep
scan for a "UID: " line.

Out of scope, each belonging to a named sibling: the verb and flag surface, UID
minting and node creation (SLICE-SDOC-CLI); refusing writes to operator-owned
fields (MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD); relocating the cache
(MECH-SDOC-CACHE-DIR-FOR-WORKTREES); trimming the format set
(MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS). fp-check keeps reading the JSON export; this changes
the write path only.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The copied order list matches the grammar today, and the wrapper will need field
writes for every field rather than one, so generalising a hand-copied list is the
wrong direction when strictdoc already owns the order. The larger gain is that
going through the model collapses find, edit and format into one path: the model
knows each node's file, so the glob-and-grep scan disappears, and SDWriter
produces the canonical form, so the "run strictdoc format afterward" step in
fp-accept's docstring disappears with it. That is what SLICE-SDOC-CLI means by
formatting applied by the same code path.

Owning validation on this side is not a tax the model API imposes; it is the
finding that makes this node necessary rather than tidy. set_field_value is a
mutator with no opinion about the value, so a wrapper that only swapped in the
API would be strictly less safe than the text editor it replaces, which at least
could not produce an invalid choice by accident.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured on strictdoc 0.28.3 against a scratch copy of this repo's nine
documents; nothing was written in the checkout.

Grammar resolution. A single-document parse leaves elements_by_type at ["TEXT"]
and set_field_value raises KeyError("MECHANISM"); after
TraceabilityIndexBuilder.create it holds all six element types and
element.field_titles for MECHANISM is exactly the grammar's list. The @repo
alias is resolved in traceability_index_builder.py:421-446 through
project_config.grammars, not by the reader.

The previous statement said the whole graph is required. It is not, and the
correction matters because the cheap path looks like it works. SDocGrammarReader
().read_from_file(...) plus document.grammar.update_with_elements(g.elements)
makes set_field_value work off a single-document parse (0.43 s parse, 7 ms
grammar) and for 99-backlog.sdoc produces a byte-identical 130839-byte write.
It drops the TEXT element the builder back-fills at
traceability_index_builder.py:447-456: on a probe document carrying free text
that the corpus exports clean (exit 0), the hand-stitched path raises
KeyError("TEXT") on the TEXT node. Use the builder.

Timings, one process, NullParallelizer: cold index build 1.44 s, warm 0.11 s,
warm rebuild after one changed file 0.97 s; three set_field_value calls 36 us;
SDWriter.write of the 2820-line, 130839-byte 99-backlog.sdoc 0.8 ms;
validate_node 8-22 us; SDReader re-parse of that document 0.37 s; the whole
set-validate-write-reparse-save cycle 0.34 s. Config construction alone is
0.67-0.81 s of every number here, which is MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS's lever.

Field order holds under abuse. On a node stripped of RATIONALE, PARENT_FP and
NOTES, setting them in the order NOTES, PARENT_FP, RATIONALE serialises them
RATIONALE, PARENT_FP, NOTES. Round trip is byte-stable: loading all nine
documents and writing every one back unmodified changes no file, and a
single-field edit produces a diff confined to that node.

One visible difference from the text editor. Single-line versus block form comes
from the grammar, not from what was parsed -- is_field_idx_multiline
(grammar_element.py:410) makes every String field after TITLE multiline -- so a
PARENT_FP written through the model is always a >>> block. Existing single-entry
values in the corpus are inline (01-tooling.sdoc:85) because format preserves
each parsed field's own flag, so re-signing such a node reflows it. Cosmetic
only: sdoc_fp.py:32 excludes PARENT_FP from the contract hash, so no fingerprint
churns.

What set_field_value accepts silently, each confirmed by a clean full export
exiting 1 where the unmutated corpus exits 0: DEPTH "totally-not-a-depth"
("invalid SingleChoice value"), AUTHORED_BY "robot", and value=None on a
required STATEMENT ("Node is missing a field that is required by grammar").
validate_node catches all three plus a newline inside a single-line choice
field, raising StrictDocSemanticError. It does not catch a "<<<" line inside a
multiline value. Only a line starting at column zero with "<<<" terminates a
block: "  <<<", "a<<<b", ">>>" and a literal "[MECHANISM]" line all survive the
round trip intact, so the reject rule is one regex. An unknown field name fails
closed but unhelpfully, as ValueError("list.index(x): x not in list").

Do not validate by rebuilding the index. TraceabilityIndexBuilder.create prints
and calls sys.exit(1) on a semantic error (traceability_index_builder.py:394,
470, 527), so it is a process exit, not an exception carrying detail.

SDWriter, SDReader and SDocValidator are not exported from strictdoc.api; of
what this needs, only TraceabilityIndexBuilder, ProjectConfigLoader,
Parallelizer, SDocNode, SDocDocument, TraceabilityIndex and DocumentTreeError
are. The module takes three internal imports, which the pinned overlay makes
tolerable and a version bump should re-check.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FILE-RELATION-EXISTENCE
TITLE: File relations to nonexistent paths export clean
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Check every File relation against the repository tree in
dev/scripts/file-check.py, built in cycle-check.py's shape: it reuses
sdoc_fp's load_index and iter_nodes, prints every finding with its node UID
and owning document title, and exits non-zero when there is one. Its argv is
the export JSON plus the repository root, a second argument neither
cycle-check.py nor fp-check.py takes, because the JSON export carries no path
for anything and so offers nothing to resolve against.
checks/strictdoc-file-check.nix runs it over the flake source beside the
cycle and fingerprint checks, which puts it in nix flake check.

A VALUE is well-formed in exactly one shape: a normalized POSIX path relative
to the repository root, not absolute, with no leading ./ and no .. segment.
Any other shape is a finding in its own right rather than a path to normalize
and then resolve. The target must be an existing regular file; a directory is
a finding. That rule is upstream's, not ours -- strictdoc's own
file-traceability validation accepts precisely this shape and rejects ./x,
../x, an absolute path and a directory with the same message -- so holding to
it keeps the corpus able to turn that feature on later.

The sdoc CLI checks only the VALUEs it is about to write, applying the same
well-formedness rule plus one stat: microseconds, not a whole-graph pass. The
corpus-wide gate stays alongside it, because a write-time check cannot see a
file deleted or moved after its relation was written, and that is the
direction the ghost problem actually runs.

Two exclusions, both deliberate and both cheap to reverse. Targets outside
the repository are not supported; no File relation names one today. Forward
references are not supported either -- a node may not name a path its own
unbuilt work will create. All three File relations in the corpus sit on
implemented nodes, so the strict rule costs nothing now, and relaxing it
later by gating existence on DEPTH is a one-line change, where tightening it
later would not be.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This node's own earlier premise -- that the parser will not do it -- is
refuted. StrictDoc validates File relations exactly this way, but only when
REQUIREMENT_TO_SOURCE_TRACEABILITY is in project_features, which this project
does not set. Enabling it is one config line, and it was rejected on three
measurements rather than on taste. It costs 12.32 s against 1.52 s on this
890-file tree, and that cost lands on every export, including the whole-graph
validation the sdoc CLI runs per write, which is budgeted at about a second.
It reports one broken relation per run, so N defects cost N runs of 12 s. And
its predicate is "is an indexed source file", not "exists": a File relation
naming a file that is present but binary is reported as not existing. A
checker over the JSON export has the exact predicate, reports every finding
in one pass, and costs 0.03 s.

The defect itself is worth closing rather than tolerating because a design
that claims code which no longer exists is the ghost problem the File role
was adopted to catch.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 on a scratch copy of the tree, strictdoc 0.28.3
(/nix/store/cck3q51nd32dpbmpkwv5xpkxxhpnr2ak-strictdoc-env), every export
into a clean output directory.

The defect, and a positive control for the same harness:

- A File relation with VALUE packages/semble/lib/doesNotExist.nix exports
  with exit 0, emits no warning line at all, and the dead path survives
  verbatim into index.json.
- Replacing it with a dangling NODE relation fails the identical export with
  exit 1 and "references parent requirement which doesn't exist"
  (traceability_index_builder.py:663). The export gate is alive; it just does
  not cover File.
- Prototype checker over the JSON export: 0.03 s, exit 1, all three injected
  broken relations named in one run. Negative control on the unmodified
  corpus: 3 File relations checked, 0 broken, exit 0 -- both on the scratch
  copy and on the real graph.

Upstream's own check, measured by adding
project_features=["REQUIREMENT_TO_SOURCE_TRACEABILITY"] to the scratch
strictdoc_config.py:

- Broken path: exit 1, "Requirement MECH-FILE-RELATION-EXISTENCE references a
  file that does not exist" (file_traceability_index.py:615).
- Accepted forms, probed exhaustively: packages/semble/lib/withGrammars.nix
  passes, while ./packages/semble/lib/withGrammars.nix,
  packages/semble/lib/../lib/withGrammars.nix,
  ../../../packages/semble/lib/withGrammars.nix, /etc/hostname, the directory
  packages/semble, and a document-relative 01-tooling.sdoc all fail.
  Resolution is a string lookup in the indexed-source-file map keyed by a
  POSIX path relative to source_root_path -- not filesystem resolution --
  which is why every non-normalized form fails while naming a real file.
- A file that exists but is not indexed reports the same "does not exist":
  measured on a .pyc and on a file containing a NUL byte, both skipped as
  binary. A plain .txt passes.
- Cost on the whole repo, 890 tracked files and 885 source tasks: 1.52 s off,
  12.32 s on, 244 MB maxrss. Narrowing include_source_paths to
  packages/semble/** drops it to 1.84 s and 16 tasks, but then
  dev/scripts/cycle-check.py -- which exists -- is reported as not existing.
  Narrowing trades cost for false positives and is not viable.
- One error per run: three broken relations across two nodes produced one
  error line, and it named the second node's.
- The JSON export is byte-identical with the feature on and off, so enabling
  it would not perturb cycle-check or fp-check.

Two traps found while measuring, neither confined to this node:

- ProjectConfig(project_features=[...]) REPLACES the defaults (TABLE_SCREEN,
  TRACEABILITY_SCREEN, DEEP_TRACEABILITY_SCREEN, SEARCH) rather than adding
  to them. The JSON export is unaffected -- measured identical -- but the
  HTML screens would be lost.
- StrictDoc's own grammar source calls this role experimental: "FileReference
  is an early, experimental feature. Do not use yet."
  (backend/sdoc/grammar/type_system.py:25).

The check's repository-root argument comes from the flake source in the check
derivation, following checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix's
"strictdoc export ${self}". That is the git-tracked tree, so a File relation
naming an untracked file passes locally and fails in CI -- correct behaviour,
but the failure appears at a distance from the edit.

sdoc_fp.iter_nodes walks only each document's top-level NODES and does not
recurse into sections. A recursive walk finds the same 3 File relations on
today's corpus, so the two are currently equivalent; use iter_nodes for
consistency with the other two checkers and fix it in one place if sections
ever nest nodes.
<<<

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-DECISION-ACCEPTANCE-PATH
TITLE: Who moves a DECISION from open to accepted, and how
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: run
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first open-to-accepted transition is made on this branch with its actor
and its trigger recorded, or the README states the path outright.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
No open-to-accepted transition has ever occurred on this branch: every
accepted decision was born accepted, and the one exit from open was
open-to-superseded. The README says a planning session grooms, decides and
slots, and also that decisions come to the operator as plain-language cards;
the skill does not say who turns STATUS. DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH's acceptance
language is about signing, not STATUS. The transition drawn in every state
machine is inferred from the enum, not observed.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A transition that exists only in the enum is the kind of gate that gets
guessed at. Observing it once settles who owns it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RUN 2026-08-27, by observation rather than by probe. The operator stated the
path outright, which is this spike's second retirement arm.

WHO: the operator. HOW: in conversation, ad hoc. There is no gate, no trigger
and no form, and that is deliberate while the system is being dogfooded. In
their words: "We are making it up as you go, pretend dog fooding its a good
system."

Record it plainly and do not build ceremony around it. This spike's own
rationale is that a transition existing only in the enum "is the kind of gate
that gets guessed at", so the value of the answer is that it stops the next
session inventing one.

Do NOT read this as covering fingerprint signing. This spike already drew that
line -- DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH's acceptance language is about signing, not STATUS
-- and that decision is unchanged: two reviews, the stack reviewed whole for
usability and flow, the pull requests reviewed for implementation choices, and
operator acceptance turned at the second of these, never before. STATUS is
conversational; signing waits for the pull request.
<<<
