[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SEMANTICS-DEFINITION-BOUNDARY
TITLE: Draw the boundary between defining semantics and this system's semantics
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Two layers, framed by the operator on 2026-08-25. The definition layer is how
the semantics of any model are defined: a grammar; the state transitions over
its fields; the gates on those transitions; the triggers that fire them; the
flows that hit them; where validators wire in and which hook point each one
belongs to; which validations run on CRUD-like operations versus commit and
merge operations; and how a grammar maps to structured CLI arguments. The
instance layer is this design system's particular semantics: fingerprints,
contracts, the ripple, DEPTH, signing authority.

CRUD and git operations blur the line. They are assumed to be part of the
semantic vocabulary and need a placement in the definition layer.

TODO: define that boundary in docs/spec where it belongs, and link code to
the layer it belongs to with parent and child relations against the boundary
nodes, so an implementation of semantics is never mistaken for the definition
of semantics.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES answered at the instance layer while the operator was
asking at the definition layer. The two are easy to conflate because the
instance is the only worked example, and conflating them is how a meta-model
ends up shaped by one implementation's preferences.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-CACHE-DIR-FOR-WORKTREES
TITLE: Put strictdoc's cache directory where a worktree hook does not scan it
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc_config.py's create_config() passes dir_for_sdoc_cache="$TMPDIR" to ProjectConfig. That exact literal is a sentinel strictdoc expands to <tempfile.gettempdir()>/strictdoc_cache/<md5 of os.getcwd()>/<strictdoc version>, so every worktree gets its own cache outside its own checkout, keyed by the directory the command ran in. That one keyword argument is the whole change. No devenv wiring, no wrapper, no environment variable, no new option.

It covers the parse cache written by every command, export, format, manage and server alike, because the expansion happens in ProjectConfig.__init__ and every entry point builds one. It does not cover the export ARTIFACT directory: a bare strictdoc export . still writes ./output/json, so the output/ line in .gitignore stays. The change stops the 3.8 MB of _cache landing under it, not the directory itself.

Two constraints bind the tool that consumes this. The cache path is keyed on the cwd at the moment ProjectConfig is constructed, so the CLI chdirs to the project root before constructing it. And each cached parse is keyed on the output directory concatenated with the input path, so a warm cache also needs one stable --output-dir; a fresh output directory is a cold parse cache and leaves behind a second full set of entries.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The cache is the only thing strictdoc writes into the checkout that nobody asked for, and it got in once already: commit 893009a0 records a git add -A staging the parse cache and cspell reporting 2591 issues over it. That same commit closed the route by gitignoring output/, which is why nothing fails today. What remains is 3.8 MB of untracked files inside a worktree defended by an ignore rule, in a repository where the standing advice is to run git add -A and where the sdoc skill has to carry a warning about it. Moving the writer is cheaper than maintaining the exclusion, and it is one line.

The $TMPDIR sentinel rather than a hand-picked absolute path because the cached parses are per-file but the dependency map is a single file per cache directory, so worktrees pointed at one fixed path would overwrite each other's. The sentinel's md5-of-cwd component gives each worktree its own directory with no per-worktree configuration to keep in sync.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 against strictdoc 0.28.3, on a scratch copy of the corpus, never in the tree.

STRICTDOC_CACHE_DIR is worse than inert. project_config.py:244 asserts it equals exactly "Output/_cache" -- capital O, which the earlier draft of this node got wrong. Lowercase output/_cache aborts too, with the bare message "error: output/_cache" and no explanation. The branch is checked BEFORE the config value (241-245 versus 246-251), so it overrides dir_for_sdoc_cache: with the config already set to "$TMPDIR", STRICTDOC_CACHE_DIR=Output/_cache put the cache back inside the checkout at ./Output/_cache/0.28.3. It is not a relocation lever, it is a way to undo this mechanism. Nothing in this repository sets it.

Which commands dirty the checkout today: format . creates ./output/_cache/<version>/ with 11 files. Only integrate_server_config and integrate_export_config move the cache into the output directory (project_config.py:716 and 755), and they fire only because the default value startswith "output/_cache"; format and manage have neither hook nor --output-dir. So export --output-dir <elsewhere> never dirtied the checkout, and the 3.8 MB in the current worktree, holding 0.28.2 and 0.28.3 side by side, is from format and manage runs.

After the change --output-dir no longer moves the cache, because the expanded value is absolute and fails that startswith guard. Measured: export --formats=json --output-dir <scratch> left ./output absent and wrote /tmp/strictdoc_cache/<md5>/0.28.3.

Positive control that the relocated cache is read and not merely written: on this 10-document corpus export costs 1.87 to 1.92 s cold and 1.32 to 1.57 s warm at a stable output directory. A brand-new output directory against the same warm cache directory costs 2.04 s, then 1.51 s -- because pickle_cache.py:127 keys each entry on output_dir + the input path. Three output directories left 27 entries for 9 sdoc files. Nothing prunes them, and the version component means a strictdoc bump strands the old tree; that is true today as well, just in /tmp instead of the checkout.

The one file shared within a cache directory is <cache>/dependencies (file_dependency_manager.py:36). It is written even by a json-only export but read only by the HTML generator's must_generate, so with formats pinned to SDoc and JSON (MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS) nothing consumes it.

TMPDIR is unset on this machine and /tmp is mode 1777, so the path is world-writable and predictable while pickle_cache.py:38 unpickles without validation. Not a concern on a single-user laptop; on a shared host, set TMPDIR.

Not proven either way: the sdoc skill's "incremental export produces false greens" gotcha did not reproduce on 0.28.3 for a syntax break -- an unknown field appended to 99-backlog.sdoc gave exit 1 on a fresh output directory, on a reused one, and on repeats, both before and after the change. So the relocation is measured not to INTRODUCE a false green for that break class, and the original gotcha is unreproduced rather than refuted. See the SPIKE proposed alongside this node.

Lands with MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS: same file, same function, both single keyword arguments. Two things go stale in the same commit -- the sdoc skill's line that "format and export write a cache into ./output/ by default", and any existing output/_cache directories, which the change should delete rather than leave as orphans of a version that no longer writes there.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-OVERLAY-PYTHON-OVERRIDE-SEAM
TITLE: Record overridePythonAttrs as the third override seam in the overlay fragment
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
dev/fragments/overlays/overlay-pattern.md teaches two override seams and a
rule for choosing between them: a thin overrideAttrs for a builder that reads
version and src as ordinary attrs, and .override on the BUILDER for a
lib.extendMkDerivation one that consumed an argument to derive an attr. It
never mentions overridePythonAttrs, which is the correct seam for a
buildPythonPackage and is what two packages here already use --
overlays/git-tools/git-revise.nix and, since SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY,
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix.

The INPUT-versus-OUTPUT rule the fragment already states decides this case
too: buildPythonPackage consumes `dependencies` and `pythonRelaxDeps` as
arguments, so overrideAttrs lands them as dead attrs nothing reads, exactly
like an overridden npmDepsHash. Add the seam and cite the rule rather than
writing a third independent one.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A pre-existing gap, not one this slice opened -- git-revise predates it. It
surfaced while writing the strictdoc overlay, where the fragment was read for
the seam decision and did not answer, and the answer had to be re-derived from
the nixpkgs source. The next Python package pays that cost again.

overlays/README.md now carries the seam in its source-pattern bullets, which
is where an overlay author looks; the dev fragment is where an AGENT looks,
and it is the one still silent.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Filed as issue #1267, self-contained, on 2026-08-25. That issue is the
user-visible tracker; this node is the design-graph record of which slice found
it. Close both together.

One of the two worked examples cited above is GONE as of 2026-08-27:
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix uses no override seam at all now, having become
a plain attrset extension of upstream's flake package
(MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE). overlays/git-tools/git-revise.nix is still the
example, the gap in dev/fragments/overlays/overlay-pattern.md is unchanged, and
#1267 is still the tracker -- the finding lost a citation, not its subject.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STRICTDOC-CLOSURE-TRIM
TITLE: Drop the three strictdoc dependencies that cost closure and are unused
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES measured six dependencies in nixpkgs' strictdoc
expression that upstream's pyproject.toml does not declare. Three of them --
graphviz, pybtex and setuptools -- cost closure; the other three return
through html2pdf4doc, so removing them buys nothing. Removing the three is a
`dependencies` filter in overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix.

Deliberately NOT done as part of SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY, whose shape is a
thin override that leaves the dependency set upstream-owned. Trimming it is a
separate, arguable change: it makes this package diverge from nixpkgs in a way
a version bump cannot re-converge, and the win is closure size on a package
every session already has.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded so the measurement is not re-run. If it is picked up, verify the
six-and-three split against the release in the sidecar rather than trusting
this node -- it was measured against 0.27.1 and the dependency list moved in
0.28.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
CHANGED SHAPE 2026-08-27, by MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE, and there is no
sidecar to verify against any more. The measurement was taken against nixpkgs'
strictdoc expression, and this repository no longer builds from it: the package
is upstream's own uv2nix build from their uv.lock. There is no `dependencies`
filter to apply and no first-party override to hang one on, so the six-and-three
split describes a derivation nothing here evaluates.

If closure size is still worth chasing it is now a question for upstream's own
flake, which puts it outside this repository and probably outside this plan.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-REQIF-PIN-HAS-NO-SWEEP
TITLE: Decide what keeps the inline reqif pin current
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix pins reqif inline at 0.1.0, because
mkUpdateScript rebuilds the sidecar from scratch on every write and would
erase a second package's pin parked there. Nothing sweeps that pin.

The failure is asymmetric and only half of it is loud. A strictdoc release
that outgrows the pin fails the BUILD, because the release's own constraint is
checked at build time -- that half is fine. But reqif moving on its own leaves
the pin quietly behind, and once the nixpkgs pin reaches 0.1.x the override
keeps shadowing it with an older release for no reason at all.

Three shapes, none argued yet: give reqif its own tracked overlay row; branch
on lib.versionOlder so the override disappears when nixpkgs catches up; or
accept the staleness and say so in the overlay. The third is what is in force
today by default rather than by decision.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the transitive-hash gap wearing different clothes -- the same
"ghArchiveUpdateScript refreshes only the src hash" root cause that forces a
Rust package to derive cargoDeps from the pinned src. A Python dependency is a
separate derivation rather than a hash, so the Rust answer does not transfer
and the question stays open.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
MOOT 2026-08-27, by MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE. There is no inline reqif pin
any more, and no sidecar for one to live in: overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix
re-exports upstream's flake package, and its uv2nix set resolves reqif from
upstream's own uv.lock at the artifact the release was tested against. All three
shapes offered above are answered by not having the problem.

The ROOT CAUSE is not moot and is not this node's to carry: an update script
that refreshes only the src hash still leaves a transitive pin unswept for every
package that parks one, which is the standing transitive-hash gap.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-README-ROWS-FOLLOW-DESCRIPTIONS-NOT-PACKAGES
TITLE: Make a package with no description fail rather than vanish from the README
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
dev/generate.nix's mkDescriptionRows maps over dev/data.nix's description
attrsets, not over the overlay's package set, so a package with no description
entry is silently absent from the generated README table. Nothing fails: not
the generator, not nix flake check, not the drift check, which compares the
generated file against the same incomplete input.

Fix shape: derive the row set from the overlay group and look each description
up, so a missing one throws with the package's name. That inverts the failure
from a silent omission into an eval error naming exactly what to write.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found while landing SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY: the package was registered in
overlays/default.nix, config.update.targets and the cache-hit-parity registry
-- three surfaces that DO fail when incomplete -- and would still have shipped
absent from the public README, because the fourth surface is a lookup table
nothing cross-checks. The three loud registries are why this was caught at all,
and they are the model for the fix.

Not scoped to this plan's subject. Recorded here because it was found here and
there is no other parking lot; move it if the repo grows one.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Filed as issue #1266, self-contained, on 2026-08-25 -- the parking lot the
rationale above asks for. That issue is the user-visible tracker; this node is
the design-graph record of which slice found it. Close both together.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READINESS-REPORT-FRONTIER
TITLE: Report the readiness frontier, not the whole closure
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
status.py seeds its closure walk with the slice itself, so a slice below
interface-settled reports itself as its own blocker: SLICE-FIRST-CONVERSION
prints "blocked by SLICE-FIRST-CONVERSION" and nothing else. Not designed yet
and blocked on a dependency call for different actions and should not read the
same.

The walk then appends every unsettled node in the transitive closure, so
SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION lists twenty-three blockers, most reachable
only through another blocker. What a reader can act on is the frontier: the
unsettled nodes whose own dependencies are all settled. The rest follow.

Fix shape: separate the slice's own DEPTH from its dependency blockers in the
output, and print the frontier with a count of what sits behind it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The readiness query is the only view that says what to work on next, and at
twenty-three undifferentiated lines it says nothing. Distinct from
MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE, which is about five definitions of ready
disagreeing; this is one predicate reported unusably.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-TEXT-ELEMENT-INJECTION
TITLE: Our grammar declares no TEXT element, so strictdoc silently adds one
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
When an imported grammar declares no TEXT element, strictdoc inserts a default
one at position zero rather than reporting anything. Our grammar declares five
elements and none of them is TEXT, so the in-memory grammar has six.

Benign today because the file is hand-written and nothing round-trips it. It
stops being benign at SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX, whose acceptance is a
byte-identical regenerated grammar: anything that reads the grammar and writes
it back -- strictdoc's own writer, a format pass, a round-trip test -- emits the
injected element too, so a naive round-trip comparison fails against a file
that was never wrong.

Decide whether the generated grammar declares TEXT explicitly, which makes the
in-memory and on-disk forms agree, or whether the byte-identity check compares
generator output against the file and never round-trips through strictdoc.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
An injected element is invisible in every artefact a person reads, so the first
encounter is a byte-identity check failing for a reason the diff does not
explain.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-ASTGREP-OVER-SDOC
TITLE: ast-grep could match sdoc files directly using the grammar we already package
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
ast-grep is tree-sitter based and takes custom languages through a dynamic
tree-sitter library. This repository already packages a patched
tree-sitter-strictdoc for semble, so the grammar half of that already exists
and is already maintained.

That makes structural matching over sdoc documents available without writing a
parser: find every node carrying a given field value, rewrite a field across a
corpus, lint for a shape the strictdoc grammar cannot express. Relevant to the
milestone that makes a tool the only writer of sdoc files, where the
alternative is driving strictdoc's own node model for every edit.

Unproven. Registering a custom language with ast-grep needs the grammar built
as a shared library with the right entry point, which the semble packaging may
or may not already produce.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while measuring ast-grep for the grammar milestone's conversion pass.
Two capabilities this repository already carries separately turn out to
compose, and nothing records that they might.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY
TITLE: The overlay that put the extractor's sources on the wrong side is gone
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
dev/fragments/overlays/overlay-pattern.md states an invariant: an overlay must
not import build sources from packages/, because that outbound edge prevents
lifting the overlay tree as a clean directory move. The two worked examples,
overlays/kiro-memory-distiller/ and overlays/mcp-servers/openmemory-mem/, both
keep their repo-local sources beside the derivation that consumes them.

The grammar milestone's layout puts the extractor's three Python entry points
under packages/strictdoc-grammar/extract/ while exposing the runner at
pkgs.ai.devTools.strictdoc-grammar-extract, so wiring the entry points into the
derivation would create exactly that edge.

overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract.nix therefore ships only the
environment -- an interpreter carrying strictdoc's dependency closure, its
site-packages on PYTHONPATH, and ast-grep on PATH -- and takes the entry point
as its first argument. It works, and it is not the intended shape.

Two ways out, both one small edit. Relocate the entry points under
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract/ and add src plus an --add-flags
line, which honours the invariant and splits the package's sources from its
generated output. Or record an exemption to the invariant for generation-time
tooling and point src at packages/, which keeps the package's four surfaces
co-located and admits one overlay-to-packages edge.

Nothing enforces the invariant mechanically; there is no check, and no such
edge exists in the tree today.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while scaffolding the grammar package. A partial wiring that works is the
kind of thing that stops being noticed once the logic lands on top of it, and
the choice belongs to whoever owns the overlay taxonomy rather than to the
session that tripped over it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RULED by the operator 2026-08-26: the invariant was read the wrong way round.
It is correct that an overlay should be the faithful conversion of upstream, but
the conclusion is not that sources must move under overlays/ -- it is that the
WRAPPING does not belong in an overlay at all. It belongs in a module. See
MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE for where it goes, and
MECH-OVERLAY-WRAPPING-BELONGS-IN-MODULES for the repo-wide version of the same
mistake.

IMPLEMENTED 2026-08-27, and by NEITHER of the two ways out the statement above
lists. Both of those assumed the wrap survives somewhere and only argued about
where its sources sit. The ruling says it does not belong in an overlay at all,
so overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract.nix was DELETED rather than
thinned and pkgs.ai.devTools.strictdoc-grammar-extract no longer exists. The
outbound overlay-to-packages edge is gone because the overlay is gone, not
because anything moved to satisfy the invariant.

packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix is now the only copy. It is a
factory rather than inline module code because it has two consumers that are
not each other: ../modules/devenv puts it in the dev shell, and flake.nix builds
it ONCE for the strictdoc-grammar-* checks, which run the same entry points in a
derivation with no module system in sight. Measured at the move: the wrapper's
store path is unchanged, and devenv's package list carries exactly one copy.

One consequence, recorded because it is what wrapping in a module MEANS rather
than a side effect of this edit. Build inputs now come from the CONSUMER's pkgs
instead of an overlay's isolated ourPkgs. That is only safe here because the
part that has to agree with upstream's virtual environment is upstream's own
interpreter, and the single spliced import (ast_grep_py) is guarded by the
wrapper's install check.

The repo-wide version of the same mistake is untouched and still open:
MECH-OVERLAY-WRAPPING-BELONGS-IN-MODULES, and
MECH-AGENTS-INFER-OVERLAY-CONVENTION-FROM-EXAMPLES for why writing the rule
down again is not the remedy.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-NAMED-CONSTRUCTORS-DO-NOT-SPAN
TITLE: The DSL's named constructors cover the common case, not the relation space
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The settled DSL shape gives rel.parent and rel.child two positional roles, and
rel.file as a bare value carrying none. The grammar allows more than that in
both directions: ROLE and REVERSE_ROLE are optional on Parent and Child, so a
role-less parent relation is legal, and File takes a ROLE even though it has no
REVERSE_ROLE production at all.

So the three named constructors between them cannot write two shapes the
grammar accepts. Both are reachable through the generic rel.mk -- rel.mk
"parent" {} and rel.mk "file" {role = "Depends";} -- and the types accept both,
so nothing is unwritable. What is missing is a named way to say it.

Whether that matters depends on whether the DSL is meant to be the readable
surface for every legal grammar or the fast path for the common one. The same
question applies to HUMAN_TITLE, which no field constructor names either.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while implementing the DSL against the settled shape. The gap is easy to
mistake for an oversight and rewrite, and the fix -- more arities, or optional
attrset arguments -- costs the property that every constructor is currently a
one-line attrset literal.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-CHECKS-NOTHING-BY-ITSELF
TITLE: DSL values are unchecked until something feeds them to the option surface
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The DSL is sugar only, which means it asserts nothing: every constructor is a
plain attrset literal, and the sole thing that can reject a malformed value is
the normalized option surface it is checked against. Measured by evaluating the
DSL with its normalized argument bound to a throw -- every constructor still
worked, including the ones building values the surface rejects.

That is the intended design, and it has a consequence worth stating. The
barrel declares a dsl to normalized dependency edge that does not exist at
evaluation time, so a consumer that imports the DSL and hands the result
straight to the emitter passes through no type at all. The emitted file would
then be checked only by strictdoc, at export, where the two silent duplicate
cases are known not to fire.

The guarantee therefore lives entirely in the consumer's option declaration.
Either the emitter's entry point takes values already checked -- by declaring
its input with the normalized type rather than accepting a bare attrset -- or a
consumer can bypass the surface without noticing.

CONFIRMED by measurement in the repair step of the same milestone, which is why
this node is not filed a second time under another name. Rendering values.nix
and fixtures/foreign.nix through `emit.grammar` directly -- not through
lib/check.nix -- produces bytes IDENTICAL to both committed files, and with
check.elements gutted to the identity function the model-equal, foreign-roundtrip
and negative-fixtures checks all stayed GREEN. The emitter reads every key with
an `or` default, so it is blind to every weakening the types catch. lib/check.nix
now exists and checks/strictdoc-grammar-surface-live.nix gates it, but the bypass
itself is still reachable: nothing stops a consumer calling emit.grammar.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while proving the DSL cannot weaken the types. It cannot, but "cannot
weaken" is a statement about a value that reaches the surface, and nothing in
the current wiring guarantees one does.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SGRA-ADMITS-NO-COMMENTS
TITLE: A .sgra file cannot carry a generated-file header, because it has no comment syntax
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The grammar for .sgra files admits nothing before [GRAMMAR] and nothing between
its keys. A leading # line fails the textx parse at 1:1 with
Expected '[GRAMMAR]', and there is no alternative comment production anywhere in
the file. Measured against strictdoc 0.28.3.

Every other generated artefact in this repository opens with a line saying it is
generated and naming its generator. A .sgra emitted by the grammar surface
cannot, so docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra will look hand-written forever and a session
editing it by hand gets no warning at the point of editing. The only places a
banner could hide are a field HUMAN_TITLE or an element PREFIX, both of which
change what the file means.

Whatever marks the file as generated therefore has to live outside it -- a
sibling README, a treefmt or check rule that regenerates and diffs, or a path
convention. Undecided which.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while building the negative fixture corpus, where the same limitation
forced the per-fixture expectation out of the fixture files and into their
README.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-JSON-EXPORT-DROPS-GRAMMAR-DETAIL
TITLE: The JSON export flattens the grammar it reports, so it cannot verify an emitted .sgra
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc export --formats=json reports a DOCUMENTS[].GRAMMAR block, and that
block is lossy in four ways. Measured on 0.28.3 against a grammar that uses all
four of them.

Every field TYPE is reported as the string "String" -- a Tag field and a
MultipleChoice(reader, author, reviewer) field are indistinguishable from a
plain String one. HUMAN_TITLE is absent. The PROPERTIES block is absent
entirely, so IS_COMPOSITE, PREFIX and VIEW_STYLE do not appear. Relations carry
TYPE and ROLE but not REVERSE_ROLE.

That rules the JSON export out as the oracle for the grammar milestone's
acceptance item 4, which compares the model strictdoc builds from an emitted
.sgra against the model it builds from the committed one: two grammars differing
in any of the four would compare equal. The comparison has to run in process
against the parsed document model, not over exported JSON. It also bounds what
the sdoc-cli milestone can learn about a grammar from the export it already
depends on.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while checking that the foreign fixture actually exercised the surface it
claims to. The export accepted every feature and then reported almost none of
them back, which is exactly the shape that makes a round-trip check pass without
having checked anything.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SGRA-SCANNED-PROJECT-WIDE
TITLE: A whole-project export reads every .sgra under the root, referenced or not
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc export . reads every file with an .sgra extension anywhere beneath the
project root, whether or not a document imports it. A grammar file that fails to
parse therefore fails the whole export, from wherever it sits in the tree.

Measured on 0.28.3 while committing the negative fixture corpus for the grammar
milestone: six deliberately invalid grammars under
packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/negative/ took the repository-wide export
from exit 0 to exit 1, on a file nothing referenced. They now carry an
.sgra.invalid suffix so the scan skips them, which works but encodes the
constraint in a filename rather than in configuration.

exclude_doc_paths in strictdoc_config.py is the configured answer and already
carries two globs for the same class of problem with .md files. Whether the
fixture suffix or a third glob is the right shape is undecided; the glob keeps
fixtures under their natural extension, the suffix keeps the config from
listing every directory that might hold one.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure is remote from its cause -- the export names the fixture, but
nothing connects "a file I added under packages/" to "the command that reads the
design graph". A session that adds an invalid grammar anywhere and then sees the
graph command break has no reason to look in packages/ for it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
A second producer of .sgra files under the root arrived 2026-08-27: the
ai.strictdoc module's generate:sgra task writes every declared grammar into the
working tree. It MITIGATES this node rather than resolving it. The task writes
through a temporary file carrying a LEADING DOT, so a crashed run cannot strand
a visible `grammar.sgra.XXXXXX` beside the real one for a whole-project export
to pick up as another grammar.

That is the constraint encoded in a filename for a second time, next to the
fixtures' .sgra.invalid suffix. Two encodings of one rule, neither of them in
strictdoc_config.py, is the argument for settling the exclude_doc_paths question
rather than an argument against it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-MULTIPLECHOICE-QUOTING-ASYMMETRY
TITLE: SingleChoice and MultipleChoice options are quoted by different rules on read and on write
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while writing the .sgra emitter. StrictDoc's reader and
writer treat SingleChoice and MultipleChoice options differently, and only
SingleChoice is self-consistent.

GrammarElementFieldSingleChoice strips a surrounding double quote from every
option at construction, and get_unprocessed_options re-adds it on write for any
option containing a parenthesis. GrammarElementFieldMultipleChoice does
neither: the model keeps whatever quoting the file carried, and the writer joins
options verbatim.

Two consequences. A MultipleChoice option holding a parenthesis, built
programmatically without quotes, is written to a file that does not parse --
verified, it fails with a TextXSyntaxError expecting a separator or a closing
parenthesis at the inner one. And a correctly quoted one round-trips
byte-stable but carries the literal quote characters inside the model's option
string, so a node's field value has to include them to match.

The emitter quotes parenthesized options for both kinds, because that is the
only spelling the grammar accepts. The residual asymmetry is in the model value
and is upstream's.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed because the emitter's quoting rule was specified as what strictdoc's own
writer does, and the writer does two different things depending on the field
kind. Worth knowing before any consumer compares an option vocabulary against a
field value, and before anyone treats strictdoc's writer as the byte-identity
oracle for a grammar that uses MultipleChoice.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DUPLICATE-ELEMENT-TAG-SILENT
TITLE: Duplicate element TAG is a third silent grammar collision
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3. Two grammar elements sharing a TAG parse clean and export
exit 0. DocumentGrammar registers each element with an assignment into
elements_by_type keyed on the tag, so the lookup keeps the LAST while
grammar.elements still holds both. Verified directly: a two-element grammar with
both elements tagged X reports elements X and X, registered_elements holding the
single name X, and elements_by_type for X carrying the SECOND element's fields.

That is the same class as the duplicate field title and duplicate relation role
cases the implementation brief records, and the same direction as the field one.
The brief says those two are the only checks that belong at Nix evaluation; that
count is one short.

packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/emit.nix already guards it in its whole-file
renderer alongside the other two. The open item is the brief's line, and the
prior question of whether such checks belong to the emitter at all or to the
type surface.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found while proving the emitter, by asking whether the two documented collisions
were the whole set rather than by happening to hit one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-NIX-REGEX-DIALECT-GAP
TITLE: Nix's regex engine cannot express the dialect StrictDoc's grammar is written in
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while writing the faithful extractor. builtins.match is
POSIX ERE. It rejects, outright and at match time rather than at declaration
time, three constructs the .sgra grammar uses: negative lookahead, the \S class
shorthand, and backreferences. Three of the nineteen reachable rules carry one:
FieldName is (?!^UID)(?!^RELATIONS)..., SingleLineString is (?!>>>\r?\n)\S...,
and ChoiceOption is (["])[^,]+\1|[^,()"]+.

So "faithful means the exact regex" and "the type is checkable" cannot both
hold. lib/faithful.nix therefore carries two spellings per pattern -- source,
upstream's verbatim, and ere, a mechanical rewrite -- plus the named rewrites
that got from one to the other and any deny prefixes a hoisted lookahead
became. Every rewrite is exact rather than widening: a lookahead becomes an
explicit prefix rejection, and a construct no named rewrite claims raises.

The open item is that nothing re-validates ere against source. A rewrite that
silently widened would pass every check in this milestone, because the only
oracle is that the ERE compiles at all.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Worth knowing before anyone reads the surface as carrying upstream's patterns
verbatim, and before the normalizer treats ere as the authority. source is the
authority; ere is a derived artifact of a translation with no test behind it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TEXTX-ASGN-ATTR-NAME
TITLE: The peg assignment node carries its attribute name, and positional correlation is wrong
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on textx as pinned by 0.28.3. A synthetic __asgn_plain /
__asgn_oneormore / __asgn_zeroormore node carries _attr_name and _exp_str, both
the attribute's name. That is the correct way to pair a peg assignment with the
metamodel attribute it fills.

The obvious alternative -- pairing them positionally, since _tx_attrs preserves
declaration order -- is wrong, and wrong in exactly one place, which is the
worst shape for a bug like this. GrammarElementFieldSingleChoice and
GrammarElementFieldMultipleChoice each assign options TWICE, as
(options = ChoiceOption) (options *= ChoiceOptionXs), so they have five
assignment nodes against four attributes and every attribute after the third
shifts by one. Every other reachable rule correlates positionally just fine.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded because a positional walk passes on eighteen of nineteen rules and
then mislabels REQUIRED as options on the two choice-field rules, which is a
plausible surface that type-checks and is silently wrong.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PEG-REACHABILITY-COUNT
TITLE: Nineteen rules are reachable from DocumentGrammarWrapper, not twenty-five
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3. Walking rule references out of the arpeggio parser tree,
unioned with _tx_inh_by for the two abstract rules, closes at NINETEEN rules
from DocumentGrammarWrapper: BooleanChoice, ChoiceOption, ChoiceOptionXs,
DocumentGrammar, DocumentGrammarWrapper, FieldName, GrammarElement,
GrammarElementField and its four concrete kinds, GrammarElementRelation and its
three concrete kinds, RequirementType, ReservedKeyword, SingleLineString.

The implementation brief says 25 of 43. The difference is the access path, not
the grammar: _tx_attrs reports an attribute's declared class, and for a value
that is an inline regex textx declares it STRING, which drags textx's
synthesized base types (STRING, ID, BOOL, INT, FLOAT, STRICTFLOAT, NUMBER,
OBJECT, BASETYPE) into a metamodel-side walk. None of them appears in the peg,
and none of them constrains anything a .sgra file can say.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Both numbers are right about their own walk. Recorded so a later session
checking the surface for completeness against the brief's 25 does not go
looking for six missing rules that are not rules of this grammar.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-EXTRACT-WRAPPER-NO-FORMATTER
TITLE: The extraction wrapper carries no Nix formatter, so generation is only idempotent by accident
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract.nix puts strictdoc's dependency
closure and ast-grep on the wrapper's PATH. It does not put alejandra there.

Generated Nix has to come out already formatted, or treefmt rewrites it and the
next --check reads that rewrite as drift. extract.py therefore shells out to
alejandra and warns when it is missing. In a devenv shell it resolves from the
ambient PATH, which is why generation is currently idempotent -- verified,
treefmt reports 0 changed and --check exits 0 -- but that is the operator's
profile doing the work, not the package.

Two ways to close it: add alejandra to the wrapper's --suffix PATH, or have the
generator emit alejandra's canonical form directly. The first is one line and
makes the warning unreachable.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed because the warning path is the one that runs in CI, where the wrapper
is the whole environment. A CI regeneration would silently produce a file that
the repository's own formatter immediately changes.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The FILE moved 2026-08-27 and the finding did not. The wrapper is now
packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix, the overlay having been deleted
outright (MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY); alejandra is still deliberately
absent from it, and the header now says so in as many words rather than leaving
it to be noticed. The workaround is still per-caller:
checks/strictdoc-grammar-surface-current.nix names alejandra in
nativeBuildInputs. Neither of the two ways to close it has been taken. Read
together with MECH-FORMAT-NIX-DOWNGRADES-A-MISSING-FORMATTER, the same defect
seen from the generator's side.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-LIST-SEPARATOR-SUPPRESSED
TITLE: A separated list's separator does not survive into the faithful surface
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3. The choice-option list is spelled

  '(' ((options = ChoiceOption) (options *= ChoiceOptionXs)) ')'

and ChoiceOptionXs is the separator-carrying rule:

  ChoiceOptionXs[noskipws]: /, /- ChoiceOption

The `-` suffix is arpeggio's suppression marker, and the extracted production
for ChoiceOptionXs carries only the rule reference: the /, / match is gone.

So the SHAPE of a separated list is derivable from the surface -- one mandatory
assignment then a zeroOrMore assignment to the same option -- and the
normalizer classifies it from exactly that. The SEPARATOR is not. The ", " join
therefore lives in the encoder as knowledge rather than as a derivation, in
both lib/emit.nix and the `commaList` pair in extract/normalize.py.

Fix shape: teach extract.py to record suppressed matches as a `separator` on
the repetition item, and have the encoder read it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The whole point of generating the surface is that no vocabulary is hand-typed.
", " is currently a hand-typed constant in two files, and if upstream ever
changed the separator both would keep emitting the old one against a grammar
that no longer parses it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-SHAPE-DIVERGES-FROM-SURFACE
TITLE: The DSL and emitter value shape does not typecheck against the generated surface
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Two incompatible field shapes are live at once in packages/strictdoc-grammar.

The generated surface follows textx, which repeats TITLE inside every
alternative, so a field is the union itself:

  {singleChoice = {title = "STATUS"; options = ["Draft"]; required = true;};}

lib/dsl.nix and lib/emit.nix follow the implementation brief's "shape, as
designed", which hoists the title out and names the union `kind`, and which
spells the vocabulary `choices`:

  {title = "STATUS"; kind.singleChoice = {choices = ["Draft"];};}

Both evaluate. They do not typecheck against each other: values built with the
DSL are never checked by types.GrammarElementField, so the brief's acceptance
item 3 -- "the DSL evaluates and cannot weaken the types" -- is not currently
demonstrable, and item 4's round trip runs entirely on the emitter's own shape.

Fix shape: one of the two moves. Either the emitter and DSL adopt the generated
shape, or normalize.py grows a converter pair that rewrites the union into
title-plus-kind and encodes back -- which is exactly what a converter pair is
for, and would make the hoist a recorded decision rather than a divergence.

RESOLVED by the integration step of the same milestone, by the FIRST move.
lib/dsl.nix and lib/emit.nix now speak the generated shape, `kind` is gone, and
lib/check.nix runs every value through types.DocumentGrammar before emit sees
it. The vocabulary is spelled `choices` on both sides because the extractor
renames it: `options` is unusable as a submodule CONFIG key -- the module system
reads it as an option declaration. Left in place rather than deleted because
removing a backlog node is the operator's call.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while checking that emit.nix consumes normalized.encoders: it does, but
it reads `body.choices` off a value the normalized types would reject. Two
shapes that both work in isolation are the kind of thing that only surfaces
when someone finally wires the check that compares them.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CHOICE-ENCODER-PAIR-SPLIT
TITLE: The choice-option converter pair is split across two files with nothing checking they agree
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
ChoiceOption is now the one converter whose two halves live in different files.
The type rewrite is `decodedOption` in extract/normalize.py, which turns the
faithful token pattern into the DECODED option value; the encode half is
`choiceOption` in lib/emit.nix, which puts the quotes back.

The split is deliberate -- when to quote is decided by
GrammarElementFieldSingleChoice.get_unprocessed_options, which lives in
strictdoc's WRITER and not in the grammar the surface is derived from, so
normalize.py has no business deriving it. Every other pair ships both halves
from normalize.py, and emit.nix merges `defaultEncoders // normalized.encoders`
so a name shipped there wins.

The cost is that nothing checks the two halves agree. If upstream widened the
unquoted branch, or started quoting on a different condition, the type would
accept a value the encoder spells in a way the grammar no longer parses -- and
the only thing that would notice is the round-trip fixture happening to contain
such an option.

Fix shape: have normalize.py record the DECODE half's inverse as an assertion
the check task can run, or move the quoting rule in by extracting it from the
writer the same way the grammar is extracted from the builder.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found by rendering fixtures/foreign.nix: `MIT (Expat)` failed the faithful
ChoiceOption pattern, because the faithful type describes the TOKEN as written
(quoted, for a parenthesised option) while the value in Nix is the option
itself. Adding the decode converter fixed it, and made the pair's split
explicit for the first time.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-LINE-ANCHORED-DENIAL-UNENFORCED
TITLE: FieldName's anchored denials are decided from a register, not from the grammar
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The split itself is now decided per word rather than per anchor, and the rule
is in decompose.py: run each denied word against the SIBLING ALTERNATIVES of
the rule that denies it, enforce the ones that are an alternative of nothing,
and record the rest. `sibling_alternatives` computes that set off the
production tree, so the decision is the grammar's wherever the grammar shows
it, and an anchored denial that is neither computable nor registered is a hard
error.

What is left is that FieldName's two answers are REGISTERED, in
decompose.LINE_ANCHORED_DENIALS, rather than derived. The rule that gives its
lookaheads their purpose is SDocNodeField, which lives in the DOCUMENT grammar
and is not in the recipe this surface walks -- the .sgra grammar only ever
enters FieldName after the literal '  - TITLE: ', which is a position no choice
discriminates. So the register carries UID (an alternative of SDocNodeField, a
backtracking guard, unenforced) and RELATIONS (an alternative of nothing, a
reservation, enforced), each with its reason.

A register entry is a human decision about a rule nobody is reading, and it
cannot notice upstream moving a word from one bucket to the other. Fix shape:
walk the document grammar too, at least far enough to read the alternatives at
each anchored rule's entry positions, and let the same discriminator answer
without a register. The discriminator is already exercised against a synthetic
SDocNodeField-shaped recipe, so what is missing is the input, not the rule.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The first spelling of this rewrite was `strip-start-anchor`, which dropped the
caret and enforced the rest, and it produced a FieldName type that rejected UID
-- caught only when values.nix was first checked against the surface. Getting
it right in one direction should not be mistaken for having a check.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RESOLVED 2026-08-26 with the operator, and the resting state was wrong. The two
denied words are not the same kind of thing, and a rule read straight off the
grammar separates them: run each denied word against the SIBLING ALTERNATIVES
of the rule that denies it. A word that appears as an alternative is a
backtracking guard. A word that appears as none is a real reservation.

UID appears as its own literal alternative in SDocNodeField, so denying it as a
value would be wrong -- a field named UID is legal and this repository declares
one five times.

RELATIONS appears as no alternative, and MEASURED it is a genuine defect: a
grammar declaring a field titled RELATIONS exports clean with exit 0, and a
document that USES that field fails to parse, because the parser reaches
RELATIONS: expecting the relations block header. So strictdoc will accept a
grammar that generates documents nobody can read, and says nothing at the layer
where the mistake was made. That is precisely what a type here can catch.

Enforcing it needs no regex and no new machinery. patternType already carries an
addCheck branch for its enforced deny list, and a denial lifted out of a pattern
is a list-membership test rather than a lookahead -- which is what makes it
expressible at all, since POSIX extended regex has no lookaheads. Verified that
addCheck fires on a scalar string type: DEPTH and UID accepted, RELATIONS and a
lowercase name rejected.

The earlier reasoning here was wrong twice over -- that the machinery did not
exist, and that a lookahead's absence from the regex dialect meant the rule
could not be enforced at all.

IMPLEMENTED 2026-08-27, together with moving the whole decomposition out of the
faithful surface. FieldName now carries deny = ["RELATIONS"] and
denyAtLineStart = ["UID"], and the type rejects a field titled RELATIONS,
accepts one titled UID, and still rejects a lowercase title. The rendered
grammar is byte-identical to docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra, which declares UID five
times, so the accept arm is not a bare assertion. What this node still names is
the register, not the enforcement.

DEPTH STAYS sketch, which is not drift against the paragraph above. What landed
is the enforcement; what this node is TITLED for -- deriving FieldName's two
answers from the document grammar instead of registering them -- is unbuilt and
has no design yet. Raising DEPTH here would report the residual as done.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-ENFORCED-DENIAL-IS-A-PREFIX
TITLE: An enforced denial rejects every word starting with it, and only the exact word was measured
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
patternType's addCheck branch rejects a value whose PREFIX is a denied word --
`builtins.match "(${d}).*"` -- so enforcing RELATIONS also rejects a field
titled RELATIONSHIP or RELATIONS_2.

That is the faithful transcription. `(?!^RELATIONS)` denies a prefix too: the
caret constrains where the lookahead applies, not how much of the input it
covers, so upstream rejects exactly the same set at a line start. RequirementType
already relies on the same reading, which is why DOCUMENT_FROM_FILE is reserved
along with DOCUMENT.

What was MEASURED is narrower than what is enforced. The defect behind the
enforcement was measured on the exact word RELATIONS: a grammar declaring that
field exports clean and its documents then fail to parse. Nobody has run the
same experiment on RELATIONSHIP, so it is possible -- not likely, but untested
-- that the document grammar recovers on a longer word and the type is stricter
than the parser.

Fix shape: render a grammar declaring a RELATIONSHIP field, export it, and read
a document that uses it. If it parses, the two denial kinds want different
enforcement -- a prefix test for the reserved-keyword kind and an equality test
for this one -- and patternType needs the second branch.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while wiring the enforcement: `deny` was reused because patternType
already carried the branch, and reuse imported prefix semantics along with it.
That is the right default, but it widens a rejection past the measurement that
justified it, and the widening is invisible in the generated file.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-SGRA-NOT-GENERATED
TITLE: docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra is generated from values.nix and CI-gated
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix renders docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra byte for
byte, and the semantic comparison agrees, but nothing writes that file. The
generation task deliberately stops at the two generated surfaces, and
check:sdoc-grammar renders to a temporary file and diffs.

So there are two sources for one artefact. Editing grammar.sgra by hand is
still possible and reddens only a task nobody has to run; editing values.nix
without running it changes nothing. Neither drift is caught anywhere near where
it happens, and neither is caught in CI at all -- both tasks depend on
interactive-only packages.

Fix shape: the operator decides whether grammar.sgra becomes generated. If yes,
generate:sdoc-grammar writes it and the drift check moves into `nix flake
check`, which needs the strictdoc closure available to a check derivation --
checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix already pulls it via nativeBuildInputs, so the
precedent exists.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Left as a deliberate stop rather than an oversight: making a hand-maintained
contract file generated changes who owns it, and this milestone was told not to
edit that file at all. Recorded so the half-wired state is legible as a
decision rather than read as a missing task.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RULED by the operator 2026-08-26: yes, generate it. In this repository every
sdoc grammar is generated, by way of the devenv module in
MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE. That is a local convention and is explicitly NOT
imposed on anyone consuming the published surface -- a consumer may use the
module, call the library directly, or hand-write the file.

Implemented 2026-08-27. ai.strictdoc.grammars.repo in devenv.nix names
docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra as its target, and the module's own generate:sgra task
writes it. The file it wrote is byte-identical to the one that was committed,
so the deliberate normalization commit acceptance item 5 allows for was not
needed.

Two things learned wiring it. The write CANNOT share an invocation with
generate:sdoc-grammar: `rendered` is an evaluation-time string and devenv fixes
every task's script before running any of them, so a chained write would emit
the grammar rendered from the surface that existed when the run began -- in
exactly the run that regenerated it. And the drift this node worried about is
already gated in CI rather than only by an interactive task:
checks/strictdoc-grammar-model-equal.nix renders values.nix inside `nix flake
check` and diffs it against the committed bytes, so a hand edit to either side
now reddens a required check.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-MID-IS-PER-PARSE-RANDOM
TITLE: StrictDoc mints a fresh MID on every parse, so two reads of one file disagree
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while writing extract/compare.py. Every node StrictDoc
builds carries a `mid`, and for a .sgra it is generated fresh on each parse --
a .sgra has no syntax to declare one. Reading the SAME BYTES twice therefore
produces two models that disagree on every `mid` in the file.

Any structural comparison of two models has to skip it. Keeping it is not a
stricter comparison, it is one that can never pass, and because the difference
is per-parse rather than per-file it does not look like a bug in whatever
produced the second file.

The consequence beyond comparison: a MID is not an identity that survives a
re-read, so nothing may key on one across parses.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The first run of compare.py against two byte-identical files reported them
different, with a mid mismatch on every node. Worth knowing before anything
else -- a diff tool, a cache key, a cross-file reference -- treats a MID as
stable.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-EXPORT-FRAMES-VALIDATION-AS-PARSE-ERROR
TITLE: strictdoc export reports a semantic rejection as "could not parse file"
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while wiring checks/strictdoc-grammar-negative-fixtures.nix.
A grammar that violates an SDocValidator rule -- REVERSE_ROLE without ROLE,
SECTION not composite, TEXT composite -- is rejected during the step strictdoc
prints as `Build traceability graph`, well after `Reading SDOC` reports the
same file read successfully. The first line of the diagnostic is nevertheless:

  error: could not parse file: g.sgra.
  Semantic error: Grammar element 'NOTE' defines REVERSE_ROLE without ROLE ...

So the headline names the wrong layer, and the line that names the right one is
below it. Anything classifying a rejection by matching the error TEXT will read
every semantic failure as a parse failure -- and a fixture asserting "rejected
at the parse" would pass while proving nothing about the parser.

The layer is still distinguishable, just not from the message: a bare
SDocGrammarReader.read of the .sgra accepts all three, and only the whole-corpus
export rejects them. That is what the check discriminates on.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Worth recording because the misleading half is the part a reader sees first, and
because it makes the obvious implementation of a layer assertion silently wrong
rather than loudly wrong.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-CHECKS-ARE-IN-FLAKE-CHECK
TITLE: The five grammar gates are flake checks, so they are in the CI gate too
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
checks/strictdoc-grammar-{surface-current,surface-live,model-equal,
negative-fixtures,foreign-roundtrip}.nix are registered in flake.nix's `checks`
output, because that output is what makes `nix build .#checks.<system>.<name>`
addressable. There is no third state: `nix flake check` builds everything in
`checks`, so these now run in CI as well, on both x86_64-linux and
aarch64-darwin.

The cost is a closure, not a runtime: `pkgs.ai.devTools.strictdoc` was already
pulled in by strictdoc-fp-check and strictdoc-cycle-check, but the extraction
wrapper is new to CI, and so is alejandra as a check input. That wrapper is
packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix, which puts ast_grep_py on
PYTHONPATH around upstream's own venv interpreter -- not the deleted
overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract.nix, and not a python env carrying
a second copy of strictdoc's dependency closure.
Every one of the five builds in under a second once its inputs exist.

If the milestone's "wires no CI" is to hold literally, the five move out of
`checks` into a separate flake output and lose the addressable name. That is
the operator's call, not this session's. It was four when this node was
written; -surface-live.nix was added afterwards, in the same milestone, and the
count is the kind of thing that goes stale silently.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded because the milestone brief says CI wiring is not required, and this is
the one place where "buildable locally by name" and "not in CI" cannot both be
true. Better legible as a consequence than discovered from a CI bill.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FORMAT-NIX-DOWNGRADES-A-MISSING-FORMATTER
TITLE: emit_nix.format_nix warns on a missing alejandra, which makes --check lie
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
extract/emit_nix.py's format_nix() shells out to alejandra when it is on PATH
and otherwise prints a warning and returns the source unformatted. The
generator's own wrapper package deliberately carries only what extraction needs,
so alejandra is NOT on the PATH a bare `strictdoc-grammar-extract` sees.

Consequence: `extract.py --check` run without alejandra diffs UNFORMATTED output
against the committed (formatted) file and reports drift that does not exist.
It fails red rather than green, so nothing is silently accepted -- but the
failure names the wrong problem, and the diff it prints is pure formatting.
checks/strictdoc-grammar-surface-current.nix works around it by naming alejandra
in nativeBuildInputs.

Fix shape: make a missing formatter FATAL under --check while leaving it a
warning when writing, or put alejandra on the wrapper's PATH and drop the
conditional entirely.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The warning is the right call for a write, where an unformatted file is fixed by
the next treefmt. It is the wrong call for a comparison, where the formatter is
part of what "current" means.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TREE-SITTER-CHOICE-OPTION-CANNOT-LEX-A-DOT
TITLE: The patched tree-sitter grammar cannot lex a dot in an unquoted choice option
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
overlays/generic/tree-sitter-strictdoc.patch now adds the QUOTED arm to
choice_option, which is the only way to write an option containing a
parenthesis and which costs ZERO regressions on strictdoc's own docs. The
UNQUOTED arm is still upstream's `[\w/-]+`, so an option containing a `.` --
`GPL-3.0-or-later`, any SPDX identifier with a version -- does not parse, even
though strictdoc itself accepts it (`(["])[^,]+\1|[^,()"]+`).

Widening the charset was measured three ways and every one lost, all on
strictdoc_04_release_notes.sdoc against a 9-error-node baseline:

  transcribing strictdoc's own classes  -> 89 nodes, 76795 -> 98406 error bytes
  upstream's charset plus a bare `.`    -> 89 nodes
  a dot only BETWEEN word runs          -> 108 nodes

The error BYTES rise with the node count, so this is a real loss of coverage,
not error-recovery reshaping. Cause: tree-sitter lexes by longest match over
every token valid in the current state -- and in an error-recovery state every
token is valid -- so a wider choice_option out-competes the narrow tokens
wherever recovery is running.

Fix shape: restrict WHERE the token can be lexed rather than widening WHAT it
matches -- an external scanner, or restructuring grammar_field_single_choice /
_multiple_choice so the option list is lexed in a state of its own. Belongs to
SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE, which owns the grammar patch.

Until then packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/foreign.sgra carries
`GPL-3-only`, not `GPL-3.0-or-later`; the dot was incidental to what that
fixture exercises and its README says so.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found by checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix the moment foreign.sgra entered the
repository's own corpus -- the check did exactly its job. Recorded rather than
fixed because a charset is the wrong lever and the right one is a grammar
restructure in another slice.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CORPUS-CHECK-COUNTS-ERROR-NODES-NOT-SPAN
TITLE: The corpus check's no-regression arm counts ERROR nodes, which recovery reshapes
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix gates a grammar change on the number of
ERROR/MISSING NODES per already-failing file. That number is a property of how
tree-sitter's error recovery segmented the unparsed text, not of how much text
went unparsed: one candidate patch took strictdoc_01_user_guide.sdoc from 42
nodes to 19 while its error BYTES fell 1921 -> 501 (a genuine improvement), and
another took strictdoc_04_release_notes.sdoc from 9 nodes to 89 with error bytes
rising 76795 -> 98406 (a genuine loss). Both directions happened to agree here,
so the check's verdict was right -- but only by luck, since a change that merely
fragments one big ERROR into many reads as a large regression.

Fix shape: report both, and gate on the byte span. The node count stays useful
as a diagnostic.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while iterating on the choice_option widening above, where the node
count was the only signal available and had to be cross-checked by hand against
the spans before the verdict could be trusted.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE
TITLE: dev/tasks/check.nix restates the flake checks instead of running them
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`check:sdoc-grammar` and checks/strictdoc-grammar-*.nix implement the same
acceptance list twice, in bash and in Nix, against the same fixtures. They agree
today. Nothing makes them agree tomorrow: the flake checks read committed bytes
through the flake's `self` while the task reads the working tree, and only the
flake side reads the layer expectation out of fixtures/README.md, so the task's
negative arm accepts a fixture failing for the wrong reason.

Acceptance item 3 is already handled the other way -- the task shells out to
`nix build .#checks.<system>.strictdoc-grammar-surface-live` rather than
restating its case table -- and that is the shape the rest should converge on:
the task becomes a working-tree render plus a deferral, and the assertions live
in one place.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Written when item 3 was added and the choice had to be made once. Deferring is
cheap for a new arm and a rewrite for the existing five, so the existing five
were left alone and the divergence recorded instead of half-fixed.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The operator asked for a position rather than a question, 2026-08-26. Position:
the flake check is the gate and the devenv task should defer to it, which one of
the six arms already does. The duplication is therefore a defect rather than a
design.

Held rather than acted on, because the boundary it depends on is not written
down anywhere -- see MECH-CHECK-SYSTEM-BOUNDARY, which is the repo-wide version
and interacts with the standing intention to move checks into packages/.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DEFAULT-EXPORT-IS-RED-ON-RST
TITLE: The default export format fails on this corpus, so only --formats=json is ever green
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`strictdoc export . --output-dir <dir>` with no `--formats` exits 1 on this
corpus, and always has. StrictDoc renders every node field through docutils on
the HTML path, and SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE's STATEMENT carries the bare glob
`*.sdoc`, which RST reads as an inline emphasis start-string with no end-string:
"problems when converting RST to HTML", raised from
rst_to_html_fragment_writer.py:231. Measured against strictdoc 0.28.3 on
2026-08-26. The prose predates this plan's tooling work.

Every documented loop in this repository passes `--formats=json` -- the sdoc
skill, docs/sdoc/README.md, the recipe in dev/scripts/fp-check.py and
cycle-check.py, checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix and checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix
-- so the RST writer never runs and the breakage is invisible. Nothing checks
this corpus against the renderer a person would read it in.

The fix at authoring time is one character, and the rule is not the one it looks
like. MEASURED by driving docutils directly: a glob inside a single backtick is
CLEAN, and so is one inside double backticks, while a bare glob warns whether it
appears once or twice on a line -- an asterisk that opens emphasis needs a
closing asterisk that ENDS a word, and a glob never supplies one. So a pair of
globs on one line is not a matched pair; it is two unterminated openings.

Three bare sites exist today: 01-tooling.sdoc:195, 01-tooling.sdoc:208, and the
unquoted `checks/strictdoc-grammar-*.nix` inside
MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE, which this milestone wrote -- all three
confirmed to warn, by a session with no way to notice. The export aborts at the
first, so the count comes from probing docutils rather than from the export.

Fix shape: either escape globs at authoring time and put an HTML export in the
gate, or record that HTML is not a supported view of this corpus and make the
JSON-only loop explicit rather than incidental. Pinning formats per
MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS would settle it by removing the HTML writer -- note that
node's separate false green, where `--formats=html` then exits 0 having written
nothing.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found by the landing step running the milestone's validation loop literally as
worded -- "a clean export to a temp dir" -- rather than as the repository's own
scripts spell it. The two differ, and only one of them is green.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RULED by the operator 2026-08-26: tech debt against this plan, LOW priority, may
sit unfixed for several milestones. Found during milestone one, which is where
it should be referenced back to.

The requirement it implies is separate and is tracked as
MECH-HTML-RENDER-MUST-PASS: whatever the fix, either repair the prose or make it
error loudly, but the operator does not want this to become a blocker on work
that has nothing to do with HTML.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-UPSTREAM-GRAMMAR-CASES
TITLE: Borrow upstream's grammar test cases as fixtures rather than extracting them
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The strictdoc package ships no .sgra files at all, and upstream's grammar test
data is multiline Python strings or objects built straight from constructors,
so there is no corpus sitting on disk to point a tool at. Building extraction
machinery for it would cost more than it returns.

Hand-convert a selection of their unit-test grammars into fixtures beside ours
instead. The value is edge cases nobody here would think to write, not
coverage: their tests were written against the parser by the people who wrote
it. Source is their tests/unit tree, the grammar-element tests specifically.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Our fixtures test what we thought of. Theirs test what broke. The two sets
overlap less than either author would guess, and the conversion is a morning's
reading rather than a tool to maintain.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRAMMAR-ARBITRARY-GENERATOR
TITLE: Derive a value generator from the grammar surface rather than writing one
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Property testing for the grammar surface, deliberately deferred past milestone
one. The shape worth building is a generator derived from the same model the
surface is, the way a schema library derives one, so the generator and the type
cannot drift apart.

Point it at the ROUND TRIP -- generate a valid grammar, emit it, parse it with
strictdoc, compare -- not at whether the type accepts what the generator made.
The second proves almost nothing, because the generator was written from the
type.

Viable: lib.mod exists and builtins.fromTOML parses hexadecimal, so a seeded
generator in pure Nix is available if that is where it should live.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The byte-identity gate covers one grammar. A round trip over generated ones
covers the shapes nobody wrote a fixture for, which is where an emitter that
handles our five node types and nothing else would still look correct.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-MULTILINE-FIELD-RULES
TITLE: Which fields may carry block values belongs to the writing tool
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A field's value is written either inline or as a block delimited by the
triple-angle markers, and strictdoc decides which from three things at once:
reserved single-line names are never blocks; otherwise position decides, with
everything after TITLE or STATEMENT being a block; and non-string types are
never blocks.

That is document-writing behaviour, not grammar declaration, so it belongs to
the milestone that makes a tool the only writer of sdoc files rather than to
the grammar surface. Recorded here explicitly UNSETTLED at the operator's
instruction, to be picked up there rather than rediscovered.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
It surfaced during the grammar milestone and was nearly settled in the wrong
place. A rule about how values are written cannot be enforced by a surface that
only declares what fields exist.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RESERVED-FIELD-DECLARATION-LIES
TITLE: A reserved field name can be declared with a type that is then ignored
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Declaring UID as a choice type parses clean, and the document tokenizer then
ignores the declared type entirely, matching UID with its own hardcoded
expression. The declaration is a statement the grammar accepts and nothing
honours. The same holds for MID.

The operator's call was that this is not worth modelling in the Nix layer: the
reserved names are ones we depend on rather than ones to ban, and the surface
would be asserting a rule about a handful of names to catch a mistake nobody
has made. Recorded so that it reads as a decision rather than an oversight, and
so a later session does not spend the afternoon rediscovering it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Five of this repository's ten field names are reserved, so the behaviour is not
exotic. The reason to leave it alone is proportion, not ignorance of it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DIRTY-EDGE-ON-OPTION-CHANGE
TITLE: A node whose inputs changed should go dirty rather than quietly stale
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The operator's, and it belongs with the behaviour model rather than the grammar
surface. Once file relations are settled, a change to what a node depends on
should mark that node DIRTY -- visibly needing reconciliation -- rather than
leaving it silently stale, with a model filling the gap afterwards.

The fingerprint machinery already does this for node-to-node contracts. What it
does not cover is a relation pointing at a file, which today resolves to a path
that nothing checks for existence, let alone for change. Scope this where the
behaviour model lands.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the point of the dirty-edge idea in the operator's semantics rather
than an incidental feature: staleness that announces itself is the difference
between a graph that decays and one that asks to be repaired.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-HTML-RENDER-MUST-PASS
TITLE: Rendering to HTML should be a requirement, with a check that is not a full render
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Make it a requirement that this corpus renders to HTML, and gate it. The
operator expects to use strictdoc's own site occasionally until their viewer
exists, so a corpus that only survives the JSON path is not good enough even
though JSON is the interface everything else uses.

The gate does not have to be a full render. Two shapes, and the second is
preferred: a light checker for the shapes known to break, or invoke the actual
parser strictdoc uses on each field and fail on its diagnostics. The second
avoids maintaining a corpus of known failures that will always lag what the
renderer actually rejects.

Matters more once a tool is the only writer of these documents, since that tool
is where the check belongs -- rejecting the prose at authoring time rather than
discovering it at export.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
MECH-DEFAULT-EXPORT-IS-RED-ON-RST is the instance; this is the rule that would
have caught it. A format nobody exercises decays silently, and this one decayed
without a single failing check.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-OVERLAY-WRAPPING-BELONGS-IN-MODULES
TITLE: Move package wrapping out of overlays and into modules, repo-wide
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Repo-wide, not attached to any milestone. An overlay should be the faithful
conversion of an upstream package and nothing more; wrapping -- environment,
flags, configuration -- belongs in the module that consumes it. That is the
operator's convention and it is not currently applied consistently:
overlays/kiro-cli.nix does its wrapping in the overlay, which is why it read as
the example to follow.

Sweep the overlays, list what wraps rather than converts, and move each into its
module. The operator wants to inspect that list rather than have an agent decide
edge cases against a stated invariant, so this is HITL at the point of sweeping,
not a mechanical migration.

One open question to answer first, because it changes the shape: if the devenv
module and the home-manager module wrap identically, does that build two
derivations, and can they share one?
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Overlays that wrap are the reason a consumer cannot take the package plainly,
and the reason an agent reading the tree infers the wrong convention from the
nearest example. Both showed up during milestone one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AGENTS-INFER-OVERLAY-CONVENTION-FROM-EXAMPLES
TITLE: Agents keep putting wrapping in overlays because the examples do
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A behavioural defect rather than a code one. The convention is that an overlay
converts and a module wraps, and agents have repeatedly done the opposite --
most recently the strictdoc grammar extractor, which shipped a wrapped
environment in the overlay.

The cause is not a missing rule; it is that the nearest example teaches the
opposite. An agent looking for the pattern finds overlays/kiro-cli.nix doing its
wrapping in the overlay and copies it. A written instruction loses to a working
example every time.

Fix in the same pass as MECH-OVERLAY-WRAPPING-BELONGS-IN-MODULES -- once the
examples agree with the convention, the inference stops being wrong. State it in
the overlay fragment as well, but do not expect the prose alone to hold.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded because the operator noticed it as a pattern across sessions rather
than as one agent's mistake, and because the remedy is to change what the tree
teaches rather than to write the rule down more firmly.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE
TITLE: A devenv module owns strictdoc's wrapping and its grammar generation
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Add a devenv module, named for the tool rather than the milestone --
ai.strictdoc, not ai.strictdocGrammar. Home-manager is deliberately out of
scope for now.

Surface:
  ai.strictdoc.enable    -- installs and wraps it in the dev shell
  ai.strictdoc.package   -- override the package, accepted as unsafe since the
                            generated option surface is pinned to a version
  ai.strictdoc.grammars  -- the intended way to configure it, and the path by
                            which this repository's own grammar file is
                            generated

Generating the grammar through this module is this repository's convention, not
a constraint on anyone consuming the published surface: a consumer may use the
module, call the library directly, or hand-write the file.

Closes the wrapping half of MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY and settles
MECH-GRAMMAR-SGRA-NOT-GENERATED.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The overlay currently ships a wrapped environment because an invariant about
overlays not importing from packages was read as forbidding the layout rather
than as pointing at the wrong layer. The module is where the wrap was always
meant to live.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Implemented 2026-08-27, all three options live in
packages/strictdoc-grammar/modules/devenv/default.nix. `enable` installs
strictdoc plus the extraction runner, `package` carries its own unsafe-override
warning, and `grammars` is a typed attrset whose `elements` is declared with
the NORMALIZED type -- so a consumer handing raw attrsets to the emitter, which
asserts nothing by itself, cannot get past the module.

`generate:sgra` is the task that writes, and it is defined HERE rather than in
dev/tasks/generate.nix so the module references no task it does not own and
stays importable by a project that has none. It deliberately carries no edge to
generate:sdoc-grammar -- see MECH-GRAMMAR-SGRA-NOT-GENERATED for why chaining
the two would write a stale grammar.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE
TITLE: Take strictdoc from upstream's own flake instead of our overlay
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc ships a flake. Cut over to it and retire the overlay written as step
zero of this plan.

The operator has accepted the consequence: an upstream flake tracks main rather
than releases, which is a change from the release-tracking the overlay was built
for and is normal practice. Worth watching rather than worth avoiding.

Check on the way through whether the two dependency adjustments the overlay
carries -- the newer reqif and the relaxed pygments pin -- are still needed, and
whether cache-hit parity survives, since an upstream flake brings its own lock.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The overlay existed because nobody had checked for a flake. Keeping a
first-party override alongside an upstream one is two things to maintain for
one package.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Implemented 2026-08-27. overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix re-exports
inputs.strictdoc.packages.<system>.default unchanged, on the same plain-attrset-
update contract as overlays/semble.nix, so pkgs.ai.devTools.strictdoc keeps its
attribute path and its store path is byte-identical to a direct build of the
upstream flake. No consumer that takes the package from that seam changed.

Both questions the statement asked, answered. The dependency adjustments are
DEAD, not merely unneeded: upstream's flake is a uv2nix set built from the
repository's own uv.lock, which pins every dependency to the artifact the
release was tested against, so nothing resolves reqif or pygments against a
nixpkgs pin any more. Cache-hit parity SURVIVES and now holds trivially -- the
derivation closes over upstream's locked nixpkgs, so no consumer pin can move
it -- and the row was kept because a row that passes structurally is still what
catches a later final-bound wrapper.

The main-tracking consequence did not bite. Main was byte-identical to release
0.28.3 when the input was added (same tarball narHash), it built clean, and the
binary reports 0.28.3. The pre-approved contingency of pinning refs/tags/<rel>
was not used.

Two things this cost, both logged rather than fixed here. The version assertion
is gone -- MECH-STRICTDOC-VERSION-SMOKE. And SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY's STATEMENT
now specifies a build that does not exist ("a thin override of the nixpkgs
derivation ... from a sidecar, with the two dependency adjustments"); only its
NOTES record the change. Whether that statement is rewritten or the slice is
closed and superseded is a contract question and so the operator's, not an
implementing session's. RULED 2026-08-27: rewritten in place, because
superseding is not expressible for a slice -- `Superseded_By` is declared only
on DECISION. That statement now describes the shipped shape, and the missing
transition is logged as MECH-SLICE-CANNOT-BE-SUPERSEDED.

One consumer DID have to change, and it is the one that never went through the
seam. overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc-grammar-extract.nix read the derivation's
internals: `.dependencies` (a nixpkgs list of derivations) and
$out/<sitePackages>. Upstream's package is mkApplication over a virtual
environment, so $out holds bin/strictdoc and nothing else, and `dependencies`
is a uv2nix name-to-extras ATTRSET -- the splice throws at eval rather than
mis-resolving. It now wraps the venv interpreter named by that script's shebang,
which is the only handle on the venv since it is not a flake output, with
ast_grep_py added to PYTHONPATH from ourPkgs. MEASURED: upstream 3.14.6 against
ourPkgs 3.14.7, ast_grep_py imports clean, and the grammar builder returns the
same 4adcdb05... grammar the implementation brief records. That splice is a
minor-version coupling and the package's existing installCheckPhase is what
makes a future divergence fail at build time.

Written before the wrapper moved: it left overlays/ entirely later the same day
and is packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix, so the path named above no
longer exists and its ourPkgs is now the consumer's pkgs. The splice, the
measurement and the coupling are unchanged -- see
MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CHECK-SYSTEM-BOUNDARY
TITLE: Decide what belongs in flake checks versus devenv tasks
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Repo-wide, and a tangent from milestone one rather than part of it. Two check
systems exist -- the flake's checks output and devenv tasks -- and there is no
stated rule for which gets what. The grammar milestone ended up writing five of
its six acceptance arms in both.

A position to argue with rather than a conclusion: the flake output is the gate,
because it is what CI builds and what a consumer can run; devenv tasks are for
things a person triggers while working, and should defer to the flake check
rather than restate it. On that reading the duplication is simply wrong and the
devenv side should shell out, which one arm already does.

Interacts with the standing intention to move checks into packages/<package>
rather than leaving them in one large evaluation test.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator did not know the repository had two check systems until this
milestone produced duplicate gates in both, which is itself the argument for
writing the boundary down.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CONTRACT-FIELDS-EDGE-TO-SEMANTIC-LAYER
TITLE: Contract-bearing fields are semantic, and their edge should move
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX still carries a Governed_By edge to
DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE and a matching PARENT_FP entry, from when
contract-bearing fields were in its scope. They are not: which fields count as a
node's contract is special meaning laid over the grammar, so it belongs to the
semantic layer along with relation target sets and the lifecycle.

Move the edge to the milestone that delivers the semantic layer. The PARENT_FP
entry has to move with it and only the operator changes those.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Left in place deliberately rather than overlooked: an agent editing PARENT_FP is
the failure this graph already recorded once.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CI-UP-TO-PAR-BEFORE-MERGE
TITLE: Bring CI up to par before this branch merges, not while proving it out
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
CI is required to merge, so this branch cannot land without it. The operator's
position for now is deliberately the other way round: additional CI tooling is
not required for the bulk of this experiment, because the point is to prove the
approach out before investing in machinery around it.

So: existing CI tooling is fine, and an agent that adds tooling without having
got that memo is also fine -- neither is worth reverting. What is needed is a
pass before merge that checks what actually gates the branch, rather than
discovering at restack time that the gates were never wired.

Do this when the long-lived branch is restacked into reviewable pull requests,
not before.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded because "CI is not required" and "CI is required to merge" are both
true at different times, and only writing the second one down keeps the first
from being read as permanent.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FAITHFUL-IS-NOT-A-CONSUMER-WORD
TITLE: Keep faithful out of the consumer glossary
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Faithful describes a transformation methodology -- how the surface is derived
from strictdoc's own grammar, and the promise that no opinion was added on the
way. It is a contributor-side term. It may well exist as a contributor-side
grammar in its own right.

It should not appear in anything a consumer reads: not an option name, not an
attribute path, not documentation aimed at someone using the published surface.
A consumer does not care which of our layers a thing came from, and naming a
public entry point after the methodology asks them to learn our build process
before they can call a function.

Low priority. The operator may move this to a repository issue rather than
keeping it here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Raised when a lower-level emitter was about to be named faithful.emit. The name
was self-documenting only to someone who had read the three-layer design, which
is exactly the wrong audience test for a published interface.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
PARTLY CLOSED 2026-08-27, by the review round on the decomposition and the emit
split. `lib/default.nix` was still re-exporting `faithful`, so the barrel really
did publish the attribute path this node rules out; it now wires the layer as a
let-binding and exports `check`, `dsl`, `emit` and `normalized` only. Nothing
read it -- and nothing needs to, since `normalized` IS `faithful // { … }`, so
`normalized.productions` and `normalized.meta` are the same values under a name
that describes the layer rather than the methodology.

Still OPEN: the word remains in comments and in the two generated files' own
names (`lib/faithful.nix`), which is where a contributor meets it and is the
side of the line this node puts it on. Whether the FILE should be renamed too,
and whether this belongs in a repository issue rather than here, are the
operator's.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-EMIT-LAYER-BOUNDARY
TITLE: Emit is the renderer composed with the type mapping, and they split cleanly
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Settled with the operator 2026-08-26. Two jobs, one composition:

  the inner emitter -- takes values that are all strings, knows the FILE FORMAT,
    and does no encoding. A pure renderer.
  denormalize      -- knows the TYPE MAPPING: a Nix bool becomes the literal
    True or False, an enum member becomes its bare string, a list becomes a
    comma-space join.
  the outer emitter = the inner one composed with denormalize.

The normalized surface is deliberately a MIXTURE, and this is already how it is
built: a shape no converter upgraded stays as the extracted string, carried
inside normalized rather than pushed elsewhere. regexPreserved records that as
"unchanged" with a null encoder, and the current file has four upgraded sites
sitting beside five that stayed patterns. So a consumer never falls off the
normalized surface -- the untranslated case surfaces there in its original
form.

The inner emitter therefore has a real caller: someone holding string values
who is deliberately skipping the type layer, which is the same role `raw` plays
one level up. It is not an escape hatch for cases the surface cannot express.

Mechanism, if the two are exposed from one value: an attrset carrying
__functor is callable AND openable, so `emit xs` and `emit.<inner> ys` both
work with no module system and no override machinery. Measured.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded because the shape was argued to the wrong conclusion twice before
landing here -- first as two code paths that would drift, which was wrong since
it is one implementation composed, then as an escape hatch for the
inexpressible, which was wrong because the untranslated case never leaves the
normalized surface in the first place.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
OPEN, and deliberately not decided here: what the inner entry point is called
and whether it is exposed at all yet. It cannot be called faithful -- see
MECH-FAITHFUL-IS-NOT-A-CONSUMER-WORD. A leading underscore reads as private
across Nix, which is the wrong signal for something supported. Since __functor
makes adding it a one-line change later, waiting for a caller costs nothing and
removing a published attribute costs more than adding one.

IMPLEMENTED 2026-08-27. `lib/denormalize.nix` carries the encode halves and
`lib/sgra.nix` the file format; `lib/emit.nix` is the two composed, one entry
point per node type, derived from the renderer's own attribute names. The
renderer takes no `normalized` at all, which is what makes "no encoding on this
side" checkable rather than asserted. Both surfaces still render
docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra and fixtures/foreign.sgra byte for byte, and the
emitter-alone arm of the surface-live check still accepts every case it is meant
to, so the split moved no rejection across the type boundary.

Two boundary calls the sketch did not settle, both decided by what each half can
SEE. The `", "`-joined choice vocabulary is already a string by the time the
renderer runs, so "at least one option" is checked in denormalize -- the last
layer holding the list -- while "this kind needs a vocabulary at all" stays in
the renderer. And the file's spelling of each field kind and relation type
(`String`, `Parent`) stays in the renderer beside the other format constants: it
is not an encoded value, it is what the discriminator is called in the file.

__functor was not needed. The inner emitter is four renderers, not one function,
so the callable-and-openable trick has nothing to apply to unless a single entry
point is later published. The naming question above is still open, and nothing
outside `lib/emit.nix` reaches either half.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STRICTDOC-VERSION-SMOKE
TITLE: Nothing asserts the strictdoc binary's version any more
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The retired first-party build asserted its own acceptance condition in an
installCheckPhase: `strictdoc version` had to print the version the sidecar
pinned, and that assertion was positive-controlled. Re-exporting upstream's
flake package took it away, because the package is extended by plain attrset
update -- `//`, not overrideAttrs -- precisely so its drvPath and outPath stay
identical to upstream's, and adding a phase would forfeit that.

So the whole class of "the seam evaluates fine and produces some other
version" is now unguarded. It was checked by hand at the switch (0.28.3, the
release main was sitting on) and by hand is where it stands.

Possible shapes, none costed: a separate flake check that runs
`${pkgs.ai.devTools.strictdoc}/bin/strictdoc version` and greps for the input's
locked rev-derived version; folding the same assertion into one of the two
existing strictdoc checks, which already have the binary on nativeBuildInputs;
or accepting that an upstream flake asserting its own version is upstream's job
and dropping the requirement.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Worth recording because the loss is invisible: the package still builds, the
checks still pass, and the only signal that the assertion is gone is that the
file no longer contains it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE-NOT-PUBLISHED
TITLE: ai.strictdoc cannot be published as a devenv facet while options-doc demands exact HM parity
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
packages/strictdoc-grammar/modules/devenv/ is imported by PATH from devenv.nix
rather than exposed as modules.devenv on the package barrel, and that is a
constraint rather than a preference.

flake.nix builds devenvModules.nix-agentic-tools from collectFacet ["modules"
"devenv"], which walks every barrel. checks/options-doc.nix then diffs that
merged devenv option tree against homeManagerModules.default option name for
option name. Any ai.* option with a devenv facet and no home-manager facet
fails that check by construction, and ai.strictdoc has no home-manager half by
ruling (MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE).

So there are three ways forward and none is free. Add a home-manager facet,
which the ruling declined. Teach options-doc an explicit devenv-only allowlist,
which weakens the parity gate for every future module. Or leave the module
unpublished, which is what happened, at the cost that a consumer of this
repository's flake cannot import ai.strictdoc at all -- only this repository's
own devenv.nix reaches it.

Nothing records the third cost anywhere a consumer would see it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while stripping the wrap out of the overlay: the module header asserted
this constraint and cited a node UID that did not exist, so the reasoning was
one edit away from being lost entirely.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-LOOP-SHELL-ENTRY-STAGES-FILES
TITLE: devenv shell -- strictdoc runs the enterTest DAG and stages the working tree
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The sdoc skill's required loop is `strictdoc format .` then a clean export, and
strictdoc reaches a session only through `ai.strictdoc.enable`, so the obvious
way to run it from a worktree is `devenv shell -- strictdoc format .`. Measured
2026-08-27: that form runs devenv:enterTest first, which runs
devenv:git-hooks:run, which runs prek over the tree and lets treefmt-restage
`git add` whatever it touched. The hook run then reports FAILED (restage's own
signal that it modified files) while `strictdoc format` afterwards exits 0.

Two bad readings follow. The failure looks like the format loop failing, and a
working tree that was deliberately unstaged comes back fully staged -- neither
caused by anything the session was doing.

Fix shape: reach the binary rather than the environment. `nix build .#strictdoc
--print-out-paths` and prepend its bin, or give the module a task that runs the
loop, so the validation loop never depends on entering a shell.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Hit while running the loop for the grammar-write wiring. Recorded because the
skill names the two commands and says nothing about how to reach them from a
worktree, which is the only place work happens here.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DEVENV-TASK-SCRIPTS-FIXED-AT-EVAL
TITLE: A devenv task cannot consume what another task in the same run regenerates
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Repo-wide, not attached to any milestone. devenv renders every task's `exec`
into a script at EVALUATION and runs the graph afterwards, so anything a task
body interpolates out of Nix is fixed before the first task in the run starts. A
task that consumes what an earlier task in the same run regenerates therefore
sees the PRE-RUN value, and the failure is silent: the run reports success and
writes a stale artefact.

Measured 2026-08-27 while wiring generate:sgra.
`ai.strictdoc.grammars.<name>.rendered` is an evaluation-time string, so chaining
the write behind generate:sdoc-grammar:normalized would have emitted the grammar
rendered from the surface that existed when the run BEGAN -- in precisely the run
that regenerated it. The two are deliberately separate invocations and
generate:sdoc-grammar's log line names the follow-up rather than running it.

The generate:* graph is largely interpolation-free today and so does not hit
this, which is why it has never been noticed. Nothing detects the shape. The fix
in any given case is either a second invocation or a task body that READS the
file at run time instead of interpolating it, and the second only works where a
file is what is being passed.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded repo-wide rather than inside the grammar nodes because the reasoning has
nothing to do with strictdoc: it is a property of how devenv builds its task
graph. The next session to chain a generator behind a regeneration pays the same
cost, and pays it as a wrong output rather than as an error.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-IDEMPOTENT-WRITER-UNCHANGED-IS-NOT-PROOF
TITLE: An idempotent writer reporting unchanged proves nothing about whether it can write
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
generate:sgra skips a file whose bytes already match and logs `unchanged`. So
does a run whose target path is wrong, whose cwd anchor is wrong, or whose
comparison is inverted. Every one of those logs `unchanged` and exits 0, against
a file it never touched, and the reassuring word is the same in all four cases.

The first run of the write task on 2026-08-27 reported exactly that against
docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra. It was believed only after DELETING the file and
re-running, which logged `wrote` and reproduced the committed bytes; that
sequence is what makes the write and the byte-identity claim one measurement
instead of two assertions sitting next to each other.

The shape is not local to this task: dev/tasks/generate.nix's `sync_file` has the
same blind spot, and so does any comparison-first writer. Fix shape, if one is
wanted, is to make the log carry the path that was compared, so a wrong anchor
prints a wrong path rather than a right-sounding word.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Logged because the failure is silent and self-confirming in the direction a
session already wants to believe: a generator that appears to have nothing to do
is indistinguishable from a generator that is already correct.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SLICE-CANNOT-BE-SUPERSEDED
TITLE: A slice has no way to say it was replaced
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Superseded_By and Supersedes are declared on DECISION only, so a MECHANISM,
SLICE, INVARIANT or SPIKE whose subject is replaced has no way to say so.

The question this node asked -- whether the relation is extended, whether slices
are collected differently, or whether rewriting in place is the intended answer
-- is answered by DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE, and the answer is "it depends on what
actually changed". Rewriting in place is correct when the deliverable survived
in a different shape. It is the wrong tool only for genuine
abandoned-and-replaced work, and the relation stays unextended until that
happens twice.

This node's remaining job is to be the LEDGER for that trigger: every genuine
case-3 instance on a non-DECISION node is recorded here, and the second one is
what promotes extending the relation from a proposal to work.

INSTANCES SO FAR: zero. The case that prompted this node was not one.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The graph records what is true now and, through fingerprints, when a contract
changed. It has no way to record that a unit of work was replaced wholesale,
which is a different event from either.

Kept as a ledger rather than closed because a count is the only thing that
distinguishes a real gap from a felt one, and this node was filed on a case that
turned out not to be an instance.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
RECLASSIFIED 2026-08-27. This node was filed after SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY's
statement was rewritten in place, on the reading that the rewrite lost a
transition. It did not. The deliverable -- an overlay keeping strictdoc current
-- still exists and still ships; only the implementation shape changed, from a
first-party build to a re-export of upstream's flake. The unit of work
delivered. So the rewrite was correct and the transition it "lost" was of shape,
not of work, and shape history belongs in NOTES and the commit, where it went.

That leaves the count at zero, which is the point: the gap is real but
UNEXERCISED, and building the relation now would be speculative.

Cost of extending, measured 2026-08-27 and lower than when this was filed:
docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra is generated from packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix
since milestone one, so adding a relation role is a typed edit plus a
regeneration rather than a hand-edited grammar file.

The one place extension is not free is the readiness query, which would have to
skip superseded nodes or a retired slice keeps blocking its successor.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AI-STRICTDOC-HOME-PARITY
TITLE: Satisfy options parity with a home-level install, and defer the rest
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
checks/options-doc.nix demands exact home-manager parity for an `ai.*` facet, so
the devenv-only ai.strictdoc module fails by construction. Operator's ruling:
add a home-manager module carrying `enable` and `package` only -- a user-global
install, the same shape packages/semble/modules/homeManager already has -- which
satisfies parity as a bucket item.

`grammars` is deliberately NOT mirrored at the home level, and this is a
judgement rather than an omission: managing sdoc grammars for a user with no
defined project root does not fit. The operator maintains their own
configuration in a separate repository, as most home-manager users do, and
expects grammars to be maintained through devenv inside whichever repository
owns the documents.

That makes this a real exception to the repo's config-parity rule rather than a
gap to close later, so the parity language itself needs updating to say a
surface may be intentionally home-absent when it is inherently project-scoped.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The parity rule exists so a feature is not quietly available in one place and
missing in another. A surface that is meaningless without a project root is not
that case, and forcing it into home-manager would produce an option nobody can
use correctly.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FAIL-CLOSED-ARM-HAS-NO-GATE
TITLE: The unrecognised-shape refusal is demonstrated but nothing runs it
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX's acceptance list includes "an unrecognised grammar shape
failing the extractor", and the mechanism works: a pattern carrying an inline
group Nix's regex engine cannot express makes normalize.py exit 2 with a message
naming the shape, against exit 0 on the unmodified surface. Nothing exercises it
on a schedule. checks/strictdoc-grammar-surface-current.nix says outright that
its --input stays the COMMITTED faithful surface precisely so a mutation cannot
raise the refusal instead of reporting drift, and dev/tasks/check.nix runs the
same two invocations and nothing more.

So the arm that makes the whole drift argument work -- upstream changes shape,
we refuse rather than emit a wrong surface -- is proven once by hand and then
untested forever.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found while verifying milestone one, and deliberately not fixed there: adding a
gate is work with a design question in it (a fixture carrying a deliberately
unrepresentable pattern has to live somewhere and be kept current), not a
correction to the node.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 in a scratch copy: one pattern replaced with an inline-group
form gives exit 2 and the diagnostic; the unmodified copy gives exit 0. Both
arms present, so a check would only need to pin the fixture.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DEVENV-MODULE-REBUILDS-EXTRACT
TITLE: The devenv module instantiates the extraction wrapper a second time
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix states the rule in its own header:
flake.nix builds it once for the checks and nothing else may build a second
copy, or a session and CI stop running the same interpreter. The devenv module
imports mkExtract.nix itself and passes its own package argument rather than
receiving the flake's instantiation, which is a second instantiation of exactly
that kind.

Benign today and not obviously benign tomorrow. Two imports of one factory with
equal arguments are the same derivation and the same store path, so nothing is
built twice while the arguments agree -- and they are pinned to agree, since the
module's package option defaults to the same overlay attribute the flake passes.
The defect is that nothing enforces the agreement.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The header names the failure it is trying to prevent, and the module performs
the thing the header forbids. Either the rule is wrong and should be relaxed in
writing, or the module should receive the flake's instantiation.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Surfaced while verifying milestone one. Contested during that review: the
refutation is that Nix derivation identity makes the duplication invisible, and
the counter is that invisibility is a property of the arguments currently
agreeing rather than of anything checking that they do.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PROSE-UID-REFS-CAN-BREAK-SILENTLY
TITLE: A UID wrapped across a line break stops resolving and nothing notices
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc format rewraps prose, and a UID mentioned in prose can be wrapped
across a line break. The result is a reference that resolves to nothing: grep
for the UID does not find the file that cites it, and docs/sdoc/render.py prints
the broken form. There is no relation to dangle, so the parser is content and
every existing check passes.

Found once, on MECH-CHECK-SYSTEM-BOUNDARY in 03-executable-rules.sdoc, and fixed
by rewrapping the sentence. A formatter run can reintroduce it at any time.

The check is cheap: scan node field text for a token matching a known UID
prefix that is split by a newline, and for any bare prefix-shaped token that is
not a live UID.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Prose UID references are how this corpus does most of its cross-referencing --
far more of them than there are relations -- and they are the only class of
reference nothing validates. The failure is silent in both directions: the
citing file loses the pointer, and the cited node loses an inbound mention it
never knew it had.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Related but distinct from MECH-FILE-RELATION-EXISTENCE, which is about relation
VALUEs naming paths that do not exist. This is about prose, where there is no
relation at all. Both are cases of the parser proving structure and nothing
proving reference.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GROOMED-NODES-STAY-IN-THE-BACKLOG-FILE
TITLE: Groomed nodes were left in 99-backlog.sdoc instead of moving
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The backlog protocol says grooming means moving a node into a numbered document
in the same plan and raising its DEPTH. The 2026-08-27 grooming pass raised
DEPTH on eighteen nodes and moved none of them, so 99-backlog.sdoc now holds a
large set of interface-settled nodes and its name no longer describes it.

Nothing is broken by this. The ungroomed query is DEPTH == sketch scoped to the
directory, so it still answers correctly, and UIDs never change, so nothing
citing a node cares which file it sits in. What is lost is the file-level
signal: opening 99-backlog.sdoc no longer tells you what is ungroomed.

The work is deciding which numbered document each node belongs to -- 01-tooling,
02-plan-lifecycle or 03-executable-rules -- and moving it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Logged rather than done because the destination is a judgement per node and
moving eighteen nodes between files in the same pass that rewrote their contents
would make the diff unreviewable, on a branch whose whole review model is the
carve-up into pull requests.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Rough split by subject at the time of logging: the readiness and predicate nodes
belong with 01-tooling, the plan-refs and lifecycle nodes with
02-plan-lifecycle, and the CLI, guard and signing nodes with
03-executable-rules.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PRETOOLUSE-BLIND-TO-BASH
TITLE: A PreToolUse deny cannot see a write made through Bash
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A PreToolUse envelope carries `tool_name` and `tool_input` and nothing else, so a guard delivered as a tool-call hook binds a tool and never a path. Measured 2026-08-27 against this repository's own guard, `packages/claude-code/lib/memory-collision-guard.sh`: fed `{"tool_name":"Write","tool_input":{"file_path":"<guarded>/new.md"}}` it returns `permissionDecision: deny` with a populated reason; fed `{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"echo x > <guarded>/new.md"}}` — the same file, the other channel — it produces empty stdout and exit 0, which is allow. A Bash matcher can only string-match the command text, so it catches an invocation by name and not a heredoc or a script the model just wrote. Any guard stated as protecting a path is therefore overstated, and the residue has to be caught by detection afterwards.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
`MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD` names this node in its NOTES as the home for the measurement, and its own design turns on it: the guarantee is the absence of a code path, not a hook in front of one. `packages/stacked-workflows/router.nix:27` records the same limit independently and rejected a matcher on those grounds, so this is a repository-wide fact about hook delivery rather than a fact about one guard.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Logged as the durable home for a measurement summarised in `MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD`'s NOTES. Positive control is the pair itself: the same script, the same guarded directory, one run apart, denying one envelope and allowing the other.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PROJECT-HOOKS-ABSENT-IN-WORKTREES
TITLE: Project-level Claude hooks are missing from about half this clone's worktrees
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Re-measured 2026-08-27: of the 23 directories under `nix-agentic-tools-worktrees`, 12 have `.claude/settings.json` and 11 do not. The file is a symlink into `/nix/store` written on devenv shell entry (`devenv.nix:163-165`) and gitignored (`.gitignore:38`), and the sandbox-stack topology deliberately never enters a devenv shell in a worktree. The user-level `~/.claude/settings.json` carries PreCompact and UserPromptSubmit hooks only, so a missing project file has no backstop. The consequence is live rather than hypothetical: `claude.code.hooks.validate-at-stop` (`devenv.nix:703`) rides that same file, so the Stop-boundary validator does not run in those 11 worktrees, and the project permission rules are absent there too. Any future guard delivered as a project hook inherits this.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A hook that was never installed looks exactly like a hook that found nothing. The count also moves on its own — an earlier reading the same day was 10 of 22 — so this is a ratio that drifts with worktree creation rather than a fixed defect somebody forgot to fix.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Logged as the durable home for the delivery measurement behind `MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD`'s RATIONALE. That node reads it as an argument for making the writer incapable of the write; this node is about the eleven worktrees currently running with no Stop validator, which is a repository defect independent of that design.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-VOCABULARY-SINGLE-SOURCE
TITLE: The Python side hand-copies every vocabulary and every walk, and the copies are about to disagree
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Four vocabularies and one graph walk are written three times each across `dev/scripts/` and `docs/sdoc/`, with nothing making them agree. Node discovery: `docs/sdoc/status.py:24` and `docs/sdoc/render.py:31` accept anything whose UID prefix is in a hand-written tuple (`status.py:17`, `render.py:22`); `dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py:52` walks DOCUMENTS then NODES and accepts anything whose `_NODE_TYPE` is not TEXT. The DEPTH ladder appears at `packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix:36-41`, again at `status.py:33` as SETTLED, again at `sdoc_fp.py:38` as READY_DEPTHS. The fingerprint placeholder is `sdoc_fp.py:19`, the literal `":0000000"` at `status.py:110`, and the literal `"0000000"` at `render.py:70`. PARENT_FP is parsed by `sdoc_fp.py:82` with `partition(":")` — first colon, as `DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE` specifies — and by `status.py:52,54` and `render.py:66` with `split()` then `rsplit(":", 1)` — last colon. Fold all five into the shared module `MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE` creates, deriving the vocabularies from the generated grammar rather than restating them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Today the copies agree — all three discovery walks return the same 144 nodes — so the cost of the duplication is zero and the cost of a divergence is silence. Two divergences are already scheduled. `MECH-SIGNATURE-REVOCATION` writes `revoked:<hash>` into the value slot: under the last-colon parsers `DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE:revoked:b2c00b4` yields the parent UID `DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE:revoked`, which resolves to no node, so `status.py` silently drops that dependency from a closure and `render.py:70` prints the withdrawn acceptance as `signed b2c00b4`. And a node nested in a `[SECTION]` is found by the two recursive walks and missed by `sdoc_fp`'s two-level one, while a new element added to the grammar is found by `sdoc_fp` and missed by the other two.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 against a clean export of 144 nodes. `MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE`'s NOTES logs the three discovery walks and says they are out of scope there and logged separately; this is that node, widened to the three constants and the two parsers, because the fix is one import in each of two directories and splitting it into three nodes would produce three edits to the same two files. The revocation divergence was confirmed by running both parser forms over the exact string `MECH-SIGNATURE-REVOCATION` specifies.

Absorbs the proposed `MECH-NODE-DISCOVERY-SINGLE-SOURCE`, `MECH-FP-VOCABULARY-DUPLICATED` and `MECH-STATE-VOCABULARY-FROM-GRAMMAR`.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-ASSUMES-CONTRACT-BEARING
TITLE: Whether an Assumes edge is weaker than the other roles
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first Assumes edge somebody wants to sign, or the first slice whose closure blocks only through an Assumes edge, or milestone five's ripple model landing — whichever comes first.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Two consumers ask the same question about `Assumes` and neither has an answer. The fingerprint side: measured over the 144-node graph, 0 of 14 Assumes edges carry a PARENT_FP entry, while Governed_By carries one on 28 of 39 and Crosses on 3 of 29. The skill's fingerprint ladder runs strictly downward — DECISION to MECHANISM to SLICE — and has no place for the same-tier MECHANISM-to-MECHANISM (7 edges) and SLICE-to-SLICE (6 edges) that make up thirteen of the fourteen. The readiness side: the closure walk ignores ROLE entirely (`docs/sdoc/status.py:47-51`), so an Assumes edge — a stated premise — blocks a root exactly as hard as a Crosses edge — a mechanism the slice must build on. Decide whether Assumes is a weaker edge in either sense, both, or neither.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Either Assumes is deliberately not contract-bearing, in which case fp-accept should refuse to sign one and say so, or it is and fourteen edges are unsigned. Today neither is written down, and a uniform absence is indistinguishable from nobody having got round to it. The two faces are one decision because both turn on what the role means, and answering one without the other produces a graph where an edge carries no contract yet blocks at full strength.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Both faces are named in committed text. `MECH-RELATION-TARGET-TYPE-LINT`'s NOTES cites this UID directly, twice. `MECH-READY-QUERY`'s LEFT TO THE OPERATOR list says "Whether relation roles weigh differently in the closure … filed as a backlog DECISION". Latent today on the readiness side: every Assumes target in the graph is already settled, so no verdict currently turns on it.

Absorbs the proposed `DEC-CLOSURE-EDGE-ROLES`.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-CLOSURE-STOPS-AT-SETTLED
TITLE: Whether a settled node terminates the readiness closure walk
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
`MECH-READY-QUERY` reaching implementation, since the walk it ships encodes one answer either way.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The closure walk passes through a settled node into that node's own dependencies, so an unsettled ancestor blocks a root arbitrarily far downstream. Measured instance: `SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE` is DEPTH verified and reports blocked, because it Crosses `MECH-SEMBLE-EXTRA-GRAMMARS` (implemented) whose Governed_By parent `DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION` is STATUS open. The alternative reading is that `interface-settled` is itself the promise that a node's own closure was handled, which makes it a terminator and shrinks every report. Decide which.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Not a plumbing question. The two walks are two lines apart, and the choice is what settled is taken to mean: a claim about the node, or a claim about everything behind it. It is also what makes a verified slice read as blocked, which is the discrepancy `MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE` records and explicitly declines to resolve.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
`MECH-READY-QUERY`'s LEFT TO THE OPERATOR list names this exact question with this exact instance and says "Filed as a backlog DECISION". Distinct from the DEPTH cut point, which that node's list carries separately: the cut point says which values count as settled, this says what a settled node does to the walk.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-EDGE-MAP-TABLE-RECONCILE
TITLE: Two settled role tables disagree on three rows
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`MECH-CORPUS-EDGE-MAP` is interface-settled and states Assumes = MECHANISM to DECISION, Proven_By = MECHANISM or INVARIANT to SPIKE, and Governed_By = MECHANISM to DECISION. Measured over the live graph, none of the three holds: zero MECHANISM Assumes DECISION edges against 7 MECHANISM-to-MECHANISM, 6 SLICE-to-SLICE and 1 SLICE-to-DECISION; zero MECHANISM or INVARIANT Proven_By against 7 SLICE-to-SPIKE; and Governed_By sourced from SLICE (12), INVARIANT (3) and SPIKE (6) as well as MECHANISM (18). `MECH-RELATION-TARGET-TYPE-LINT` takes the union, reading `MECH-CORPUS-EDGE-MAP` as a forecast of how one unconverted corpus will be converted rather than as a graph-wide contract. Decide whether that reading stands, and if it does, whether per-plan profiles are a thing the lint supports at all.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The reading is not free. The narrow Assumes rule was justified as cycle avoidance, and taking the union drops that constraint — safe only because `MECH-CYCLE-CHECK` now detects cycles in CI, which it did not when the narrow rule was written. `MECH-CORPUS-EDGE-MAP`'s STATEMENT is a contract field on a fingerprinted node, so the edit is the operator's and cannot be made here.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Cited by UID in `MECH-RELATION-TARGET-TYPE-LINT`'s NOTES and again in its LEFT TO THE OPERATOR list. Counts measured 2026-08-27 against a clean export of 144 nodes, 92 Parent relations.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FLOW-TEST-GIT-CHECKPOINTS
TITLE: A flow whose gates read git cannot be tested the way the existing checks are
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Every check the design system has today is a pure function of the JSON export. `checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix` and `checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix` both run over `${self}`, a flake source store path carrying no `.git`, and neither `dev/scripts/fp-check.py` nor `dev/scripts/cycle-check.py` contains the string git or subprocess — 0 matches each, against 6 for `checks/doubled-words.py` with the same pattern. `SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING` places checkpoints that are not pure: the pre-commit hook runs against the previous commit and the CI step against the point where the branch left main. A contract test for any flow reaching one of those needs a synthetic git repository built inside the derivation — commits, a branch point, and a working tree the tool can write into — or that flow is declared untestable in the harness and reported as such. Decide which, and if a synthetic repository is the answer, decide whether it is built once per check or once per flow.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
`MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT` makes a flow's declared outcome its contract and gates `verified` on a test that runs in `nix flake check`. A flow whose gates read git would sit permanently below `verified` with no stated reason, which turns the ladder's most useful query into one with a silent exemption.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Cited by UID in `MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT`'s NOTES — "Logged as MECH-FLOW-TEST-GIT-CHECKPOINTS rather than solved here" — which also carries the grep and its positive control.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-CLI-COVERS-STRICTDOC-SILENCE
TITLE: The tool must supply the two failure behaviours strictdoc's CLI does not have
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Two silent failures in strictdoc's own command line have to be closed by the wrapper, because neither can be closed upstream through configuration. First, asking for an output no registered format claims succeeds and writes nothing: with formats pinned, html, rst, markdown, excel and the bare default all exit 0 with zero non-cache files, and `--formats=html` additionally spends 0.53 s compiling Jinja templates first, because `ExportAction` gates template construction on the REQUESTED handle while dispatching on the REGISTERED one. The `--formats` allowlist is the hardcoded `EXPORT_FORMATS` list at `commands/export.py:17` and a project config can only append to it (`:88`), never remove, so no configuration makes an unregistered handle an argparse error. Second, strictdoc prints its parse and semantic errors to STDOUT beside the progress log, so the natural wrapper shape `strictdoc format . > /dev/null` exits 1 with no output at all. The tool `SLICE-SDOC-CLI` builds must refuse any handle the loaded config does not register, before invoking strictdoc, and must redirect strictdoc's stdout to stderr rather than discarding it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
`MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS` says of the first failure that "making that loud is owed and is not this node's to build", and names the tool as its home; this node is that owed work, with the stdout failure folded in because it is the same wrapper, the same invocation and the same class — a strictdoc command that fails usefully only if the caller does the work.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Both measurements are already recorded: the false green in `MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS`'s NOTES, the stdout behaviour in `MECH-TREEFMT-SDOC`'s NOTES under THE DIAGNOSTIC GOES TO STDOUT. Neither is live today — `strictdoc_config.py` currently registers no formats, so the stock list still renders html — so the first arms exactly when `MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS` lands. Exposure meanwhile is a human or agent running a documented recipe from memory without `--formats=json` and reading exit 0 as success; both existing gates read their index.json immediately after exporting and die on its absence, so neither can pass on an empty export.

Absorbs the proposed `MECH-UNREGISTERED-HANDLE-MUST-FAIL` and `MECH-STRICTDOC-ERRORS-ON-STDOUT`.
<<<

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-INCREMENTAL-EXPORT-FALSE-GREEN
TITLE: Does incremental export still produce a false green, and can one stable output directory replace the clean-directory rule
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: unrun
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A break class is named that reproduces the false green on 0.28.3, or the gotcha is refuted in writing and the export output directory becomes one stable path per checkout, written down where every caller reads it.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The sdoc skill states that a broken input can exit 1 and then exit 0 on a re-run against the same output directory, and every gate in this plan is shaped around it: `dev/scripts/fp-check.py` and `dev/scripts/cycle-check.py` both instruct `rm -rf output` before exporting. It did not reproduce on 2026-08-27 against strictdoc 0.28.3. Appending an unknown field to `99-backlog.sdoc` gave exit 1 on a fresh output directory, on a second fresh directory with a warm relocated cache, and on three consecutive runs against a reused directory; the restored file gave exit 0. That is one break class only, so the gotcha is unreproduced rather than refuted — the original may have needed a different break, a different strictdoc version, or the HTML generator's dependency map, which a json-only export writes but never reads. Decide which, and settle the output directory in the same answer: each cached parse is keyed on the output directory concatenated with the input path (`pickle_cache.py:127`), so a fresh directory is a cold cache whatever the cache directory is — measured, 2.04 s against 1.51 s warm, with three distinct output directories leaving 27 entries for 9 sdoc files and nothing pruning them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The two questions are one answer. If the false green is real, the clean-directory discipline is load-bearing and the cold parse is the price. If it is refuted, that discipline costs about 0.5 s per gate invocation for nothing, and the callers can converge on one path — they do not agree today, with the two check scripts documenting `--output-dir output` inside the checkout, `dev/tasks/check.nix` exporting into a temporary project, and sessions exporting into ad-hoc scratch directories.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
`MECH-SDOC-CACHE-DIR-FOR-WORKTREES`'s NOTES records the non-reproduction and points at this spike; its STATEMENT already binds the tool to one stable `--output-dir` for the warm-cache reason, so the retirement of the clean-directory rule is what remains unsettled. A negative result here needs a positive control: show the harness detecting a false green on some break class before reporting that no break class produces one.

Absorbs the proposed `MECH-SDOC-STABLE-OUTPUT-DIR`.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AUTHORED-BY-REWRITE-DRIFT
TITLE: An agent rewriting a human-authored statement leaves the key on words the operator did not write
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD` watches the human value appearing on a node. Nothing watches the statement underneath an existing human value changing. Measured over the branch's 69 sdoc-touching commits by parsing every node at every revision: 36 nodes had their STATEMENT changed after creation, and 10 of those carried `AUTHORED_BY: human` on both sides of the edit — `DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN`, `MECH-PLAN-BEADS-SNAPSHOT`, `MECH-TREEFMT-SDOC`, `MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD`, `INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS`, `MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD`, `MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE`, `MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS`, `MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS`, `MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT`. Six of the ten were rewritten in one commit, 91ae9322. That is 10 of the 31 human-authored nodes in a 144-node graph. Three shapes exist and the choice is the operator's: hash the statement into the key so a rewrite reads as stale, drop the key to llm on any rewrite by an agent, or forbid the rewrite and require the operator to restate.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Which shape applies depends on whether the key claims the words or the origin of the node, and that is a semantics question rather than a plumbing one. The count is not a backlog of past mistakes: grooming sessions rewrite human-authored statements routinely and the corpus says so in its own voice — `MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT`'s NOTES records that its key still reads human while "everything after them was written by a model", flagged rather than corrected because only the operator turns that key.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 by replaying `git log --reverse -- '*.sdoc'` and diffing each node's STATEMENT between consecutive revisions; the same walk detects the 26 llm-node rewrites, so a zero for human nodes would have been a real absence. The guard `MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD` builds sees this case and passes it: it classifies by what the baseline said about the key, and the key did not move.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-NODE-CODE-LINKAGE-IS-PROSE
TITLE: The link between a node and its code is prose in both directions, and unchecked in both
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Nothing joins a node to the code it describes. Outward, three File relations exist in the whole 144-node graph, on two nodes, all naming implementation files — `packages/semble/lib/withGrammars.nix` twice and `packages/semble/patches/extra-grammars.patch` — against 15 nodes at implemented and 3 at verified. `SLICE-GRAPH-CHECKS` is verified and `checks/strictdoc-cycle-check.nix` exists for it with no edge between them; `SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX` is implemented against five flake checks and a package directory with no edge to any of them. So the claim that code landed is prose in NOTES, and the query `DEPTH == implemented` cannot be joined to what it implemented. Inward, measured over the git-tracked non-.sdoc tree: 67 UID citations in 34 files, 61 of them naming a node under `docs/plans/` and 27 distinct plan nodes cited, plus two tokens that resolve to nothing at all — `MECH-SAMPLE` and `SLICE-UID`. Two mechanisms are wanted and they share one input: require a File edge to the implementing file or check at implemented, and lint the source-comment citations against the same UID-to-file map `MECH-PLAN-REFS-CHECK` builds.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The inward half arms on a date nobody chooses. None of the 67 is an edge today, so none can dangle; all 61 plan-node citations become inbound violations of `INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS` on the day a source-comment extractor exists, which is `DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION`'s retirement condition, and a dangling Parent relation is a hard export error. The lint needs no extractor and is a grep plus a map lookup. The outward half is what makes the requirement worth stating at all: a File relation whose VALUE names a path that does not exist exports with exit 0 (`MECH-FILE-RELATION-EXISTENCE`), so requiring the edge means nothing without that check beside it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27. `MECH-PLAN-REFS-CHECK` puts the source-annotation arm explicitly out of scope with a reason — strictdoc_config.py sets no source root and `@relation` comments never reach the JSON export — so this is unowned rather than deferred. `MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT` scopes its verified requirement to flows deliberately, noting that the same rule applied to every node would invalidate both then-verified nodes on the day it landed; the same corpus-first order applies here. Not decided here: whether the 61 existing citations are remediated or grandfathered until the extractor lands.

Absorbs the proposed `MECH-ANNOTATION-TARGET-DURABILITY` and `MECH-NODE-TO-CODE-EDGE-COVERAGE`.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-ENTRY-RETIREMENT
TITLE: Retire PARENT_FP entries whose parent or relation is gone
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
fp-accept rewrites a node's whole PARENT_FP field from a merge of the existing entries and the newly signed ones, and never removes anything: `dev/scripts/fp-accept.py:71` reads the existing entries into a dict and `:82` only ever assigns into it. An entry whose parent has been deleted, or whose backing relation has been dropped, is therefore carried forward on every subsequent sign. `MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT` detects both cases as fp-check's deleted and unbacked findings, so they fail the gate and stay failing until someone hand-edits the field — which the skill's third hard rule forbids. The writer needs a retirement path: drop an entry whose parent is absent from the export, and drop one with no backing Parent relation, in the same write that signs the rest, reporting each removal.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Spun out of `MECH-SIGNATURE-REVOCATION`, whose sketch statement cited the retention as part of the revocation problem. It is a separate defect and must not be fixed with one rule: for a revoked entry, permanence is the property being bought, so any pruning must skip the revoked case explicitly. Without a retirement path the only way out of a failing gate is the edit the rules forbid, which is how a hard rule gets broken by someone with no other option.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Inert today — 31 fingerprint entries, all the 0000000 placeholder, 0 suspect — and arms with the first real signature. `MECH-SIGNATURE-REVOCATION`'s NOTES already records the adjacent constraint from the other side: a revoked entry is not collectible lineage.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-PACKET-BUDGET
TITLE: What goes in a context packet, and what happens when it does not fit
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`ready.py --packet` renders a whole closure as one brief and nothing bounds it. Measured over the current export: the deepest closure is `SLICE-INSTANCE-SEMANTICS-MIGRATION` at 34 of 144 nodes carrying 207,174 characters of field text against 365,154 for the whole graph — 57 percent of the corpus in one packet. Restricting the packet to the contract fields — UID, TITLE, STATEMENT, DEPTH, STATUS, RETIRES_ON, AUTHORED_BY — cuts the same closure to 77,039 characters, a 63 percent reduction, because NOTES and RATIONALE carry most of the bytes. Decide whether NOTES and RATIONALE belong in a packet at all, given fp-check already excludes them from the contract hash, and decide what the packet does when a budget is exceeded: truncate at a hop distance, drop non-contract fields, or refuse.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The packet is what hands an implementing session its brief, so its content is spent on every session that uses it, and the lever is measurable rather than stylistic. `MECH-READY-QUERY` settles the order — dependency-first — and the record shape, and says nothing about content or size; at 144 nodes the whole-closure packet fits a session, and it is already more than half the graph.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Measured 2026-08-27 by summing every string field per node over a clean export, which is a different basis from the 59,740 characters `MECH-READY-QUERY`'s NOTES records for the same slice through `render.py`; the ratio between the two bases is the measurement that matters here, not either absolute.
<<<

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-RIPPLE-SELECTOR-AST
TITLE: Measure whether a typed selector AST beats a field-level contract flag for the ripple
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: unrun
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Both mechanisms are run over the same instance model and either the AST derives a materially better invalidation set at acceptable cost, or the flag stands and the AST is declined in writing.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
`SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL` derives invalidation from what a transition writes intersected with what a gate reads, at the granularity of a per-field contract flag, and records that the alternative — a typed selector AST — is the largest claim in the design with neither its tractability nor its over-approximation ever measured. The flag mechanism is the one with numbers behind it: it runs in 0.022 s and rejected both injected lies. This spike measures the AST on the same corpus and reports three things — whether it derives invalidation edges the flag cannot, at what evaluation cost, and how often it over-approximates. The comparison must run over one instance model so the two answers are the same question asked twice.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The slice settles on the flag and says flipping costs a type edit plus a regenerate, so this is cheap to run late and expensive to skip: an unmeasured alternative recorded as unmeasured is an argument that comes back every time the ripple is touched. Measuring it once ends that, whichever way it lands.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The corpus this runs against is the graph in this repository — 144 nodes, 92 Parent relations, 31 fingerprint entries — and the field catalogue the flag reads is `packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix`. State every measurement in this node when it is run; no result may cite a document outside this repository, since a reader of this graph cannot open one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL-VERSIONING
TITLE: The behaviour model stamps a version and states no rule about it
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL` emits a neutral JSON carrying a schema version and a model version, and that JSON is the interchange form the tool and the validators consume because Python cannot evaluate Nix. No rule says what either version is compared against, or what happens when a consumer reads a model produced against a different grammar. Decide what the stamp is compared to, and what fails when they disagree.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The grammar surface has the analogous problem in the opposite direction and answers it with a currency check that regenerates and diffs; the behaviour model has no upstream to be current against, so its compatibility has to be declared rather than derived. A consumer reading a mismatched model has no way to notice, which is the failure mode the whole milestone exists to remove.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Named in `SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL`'s NOTES as "the one piece of plumbing here with no measured precedent" — the stamp is in the emitted JSON, the compatibility rule is not. Filed so the gap survives that slice moving to implemented, at which point its NOTES stop being a worklist.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-STOP-HOOK-LIST-IS-HARDCODED
TITLE: A new prek hook does not reach the Stop checkpoint unless a second file is edited
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
`lib/validate-at-stop.sh:121` runs a hardcoded list of hook ids — `cspell deadnix shellcheck statix` — rather than deriving them from the git-hooks suite it mirrors. A hook added to `devenv.nix` git-hooks.hooks therefore runs at commit time and silently never runs at the Stop boundary. The comment at `:119-120` asks for the two to be kept in sync and is the only thing keeping them in sync. Derive the list from the same evaluation that defines the hooks, or make the divergence a check.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure is invisible in both directions: the new hook passes at commit, the Stop hook reports nothing, and neither surface says a check was skipped. This is the same class as `MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE` — one list written twice with a comment as the enforcement — in a file that every session's hand-back passes through.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
`SLICE-CHECKPOINT-WIRING`'s NOTES already carries the consequence for its own validators: wiring one into the prek suite is two edits, not one. That instruction fixes the instance and leaves the mechanism; this node is the mechanism.
<<<
